  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate says they need 'more support' but gives no detail. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what is difficult, listen to the answer, and clarify what kind of help would be useful.$qb$, $qb$Ask where they are getting stuck and listen before suggesting help.$qb$, $qb$Offer to take over one part before understanding the problem.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to speak to their manager because the request is vague.$qb$, $qb$Assume they simply need more time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a task is 'not working' but gives no details. What is the best first response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what they expected, what happened, and when the problem started$qb$, $qb$Ask them to show you the problem$qb$, $qb$Suggest the most common fix$qb$, $qb$Tell them to try the task again$qb$, $qb$Assume you know what they mean$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate explains why a deadline slipped, but you hear two different causes. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check that you understood both causes and ask which one had the larger impact$qb$, $qb$Summarize the two causes and ask one follow-up question$qb$, $qb$Focus on the cause that sounds most likely$qb$, $qb$Offer a solution before checking the details$qb$, $qb$Decide the teammate is making excuses$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they need 'more support' on a task. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what part is difficult and what kind of support would help$qb$, $qb$Ask them to describe where they are stuck$qb$, $qb$Offer to take over part of the task$qb$, $qb$Tell them to ask their manager for support$qb$, $qb$Assume they need more time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate explains a delay and mentions both a technical problem and a late input. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Summarize both factors and ask which one is still blocking progress$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge both factors and ask what help is needed now$qb$, $qb$Focus on the technical problem because it sounds more serious$qb$, $qb$Tell them how to avoid delays next time$qb$, $qb$Assume the late input was the real cause$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the primary indicator of active listening during a feedback session with a key internal partner?$qb$, $qb$Paraphrasing the speaker's main points in your own words to verify mutual understanding before proposing solutions.$qb$, $qb$Taking exhaustive written notes continuously while the partner speaks to capture every spoken word.$qb$, $qb$Nodding attentively throughout the discussion and thanking the partner for sharing their perspective.$qb$, $qb$Offering immediate explanations for highlighted operational gaps to clarify context as soon as issues are raised.$qb$, $qb$Remaining silent throughout the conversation and waiting to address raised points in a follow-up email.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A long-serving executive calls a governance change 'unworkable' but becomes quiet when asked for alternatives. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore the failure they foresee and the experience behind it, then test your understanding before debating solutions.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regional president says, 'I can live with the plan,' but their tone suggests unresolved concern. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore what remains difficult, reflect it accurately, and distinguish reluctant commitment from an unresolved risk.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a capital review, a quiet executive repeatedly asks narrow questions about liquidity while others debate growth. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Explore what risk their questions are pointing to, reflect it back, and test whether the group is overlooking a constraint before returning to the growth debate.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During a board discussion, the chief risk officer repeatedly asks about a minor assumption while saying little about the headline proposal. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore why that assumption matters to them and what consequence they foresee, then test whether it exposes a material risk the group has missed.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$At first, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During an investment review, a normally supportive executive keeps asking whether customers can reverse commitments. The financial case remains strong. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore why reversibility matters and whether it exposes a hidden assumption about demand quality before returning to the financial case.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business president repeatedly asks whether a transformation can be reversed, even though everyone else is discussing implementation speed. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore the consequence they fear if reversal is difficult, test whether it reveals an overlooked strategic risk, and only then return to implementation choices.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if risk or cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer explains a problem, then pauses and adds an important detail. You must use the information independently after the conversation. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Let them finish, then summarize both the problem and the new detail$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the new detail and ask one focused question$qb$, $qb$Respond to the first problem because it was mentioned first$qb$, $qb$Start giving a solution as soon as you recognize the issue$qb$, $qb$Ignore the added detail because it came late$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague describes a recurring problem, but their proposed solution does not seem to address the cause. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Summarize the problem you heard, ask questions about the cause, and only then discuss possible solutions$qb$, $qb$Check your understanding of the problem and ask what led them to their proposed solution$qb$, $qb$Explain why their proposed solution may not work$qb$, $qb$Suggest a better solution based on your experience$qb$, $qb$Let them implement their solution because they know the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a process is 'unfair' and gives several examples from recent weeks. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reflect the pattern you heard, ask what outcome feels unfair, and clarify the facts before judging the process$qb$, $qb$Summarize the examples and ask which one best represents the problem$qb$, $qb$Explain why the process was designed that way$qb$, $qb$Offer a solution to the most recent example$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise the concern formally instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you instructions, then says, 'The last step is the most important.' You are expected to use the handover independently after this discussion. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm what the last step requires and why it matters before starting$qb$, $qb$Repeat the last step in your own words and check it$qb$, $qb$Write down the last step carefully$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the last step and ignore the earlier ones$qb$, $qb$Start immediately without checking your understanding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate sends you a task request, but the deadline is missing. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask for the deadline before planning the task$qb$, $qb$Start the task and ask about timing later$qb$, $qb$Assume it is due by the end of the week$qb$, $qb$Wait until the teammate sends another message$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request because it is incomplete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to tell two teams that a delivery time has changed. Which message is most useful?$qb$, $qb$State the new time, who is affected, and any action needed in one clear message.$qb$, $qb$State the new time and ask people to adjust their plans.$qb$, $qb$Say the timing changed and promise more detail later.$qb$, $qb$Tell only your own team and assume they will pass it on.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone asks about the changed time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manager asks for an update while one important check is still unfinished. What is the clearest reply?$qb$, $qb$Say what is complete, name the remaining check, and give a realistic update time.$qb$, $qb$Say the work is nearly complete and give the expected finish time.$qb$, $qb$Say you are still working on it without explaining what remains.$qb$, $qb$Promise it will be finished soon even though timing is uncertain.$qb$, $qb$Say it is complete because most of the work is done.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to tell three teammates that a delivery date has changed. Which message is clearest?$qb$, $qb$The delivery is now due Tuesday at 2 PM; please update your dependent tasks$qb$, $qb$The delivery has moved to Tuesday; please adjust your plans$qb$, $qb$The delivery date has changed; more details will follow$qb$, $qb$There may be a small timing change this week$qb$, $qb$Do not mention the change until someone asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manager asks, 'Will the file be ready today?' You know one check is still pending. Which reply is best?$qb$, $qb$The file should be ready by 5 PM after I complete the final check; I will alert you if that changes$qb$, $qb$I expect to send it today after one remaining check$qb$, $qb$It is almost ready and should be sent today$qb$, $qb$Yes, it will probably be ready$qb$, $qb$Yes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are handing over a task before leave. Which message gives the next person the clearest start?$qb$, $qb$The draft is in Folder X; Priya must confirm the price by noon, then send version 3 to the client$qb$, $qb$The draft is in Folder X; price confirmation is still needed before sending$qb$, $qb$The draft is nearly ready; please check the price and send it$qb$, $qb$Please finish the task while I am away$qb$, $qb$Everything is in the folder$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need to correct a misunderstanding in a group chat. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$To clarify: the client call is Thursday at 11 AM, not Wednesday; please use the updated calendar invite$qb$, $qb$The client call is Thursday at 11 AM; the earlier message had the wrong day$qb$, $qb$Correction: the client call is Thursday$qb$, $qb$Please check the calendar because the timing changed$qb$, $qb$Ignore my earlier message$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague to correct a file before a client call. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM and let me know when done; the 3 PM client call uses this file$qb$, $qb$Please correct rows 12–15 by 2 PM for the client call$qb$, $qb$Please correct the file before the client call$qb$, $qb$There are errors in the file that need attention$qb$, $qb$Please fix this urgently$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks when an issue will be fixed, but the exact time is not known. Which reply is clearest?$qb$, $qb$We are checking the cause now; I will update you by 2 PM even if the fix is still in progress$qb$, $qb$We are working on it and I will give you another update by 2 PM$qb$, $qb$We expect progress today and will keep you informed$qb$, $qb$It should be fixed soon$qb$, $qb$We do not know$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which communication strategy is most effective when briefing cross-departmental stakeholders on a complex workflow update?$qb$, $qb$Structuring the update around core operational outcomes, providing validated timeline milestones, and offering a summary sheet for quick reference.$qb$, $qb$Sending a detailed narrative document explaining every procedural step to ensure full technical transparency across all teams.$qb$, $qb$Delivering a brief verbal summary during a routine status call and taking note of any immediate questions raised.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding raw workflow documentation files to stakeholders and asking them to review relevant sections independently.$qb$, $qb$Delaying the briefing until all secondary project sub-tasks are fully completed to avoid sharing partial updates.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit asks for an immediate public statement after a service disruption, but facts are still changing and Legal has not confirmed whether customer data was affected. What should you communicate now?$qb$, $qb$State what is confirmed, what remains under investigation, when the next update will come, and avoid claims that may later prove false.$qb$, $qb$Share the confirmed operational facts and say that further details will follow after review.$qb$, $qb$Use the most likely explanation but label it as provisional so stakeholders have something useful.$qb$, $qb$Delay all communication until every material fact is verified.$qb$, $qb$Reassure customers that no serious impact occurred unless evidence proves otherwise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A restructuring must be announced before all role impacts are known, and rumours are driving resignations. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$State what is decided, what remains open, why the change is occurring, how decisions will be made, and when people will hear more.$qb$, $qb$Share confirmed decisions and a timetable, but avoid unresolved issues.$qb$, $qb$Wait until most individual impacts are known before communicating.$qb$, $qb$Give managers a script and let them decide what to disclose.$qb$, $qb$Reassure employees disruption will be limited despite the uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board update must explain why a profitable division is losing strategic customers although headline KPIs remain strong. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Lead with the contradiction, isolate decision-relevant evidence, distinguish fact from interpretation, and state the choices now required.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues begin arguing in a meeting about whose instructions were followed. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause the blame discussion, establish what each person understood, and agree the immediate correction and future handoff.$qb$, $qb$Let each colleague explain their understanding before deciding the next step.$qb$, $qb$Choose the explanation that sounds more likely and move on.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to settle the disagreement immediately.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion without resolving the work issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate sends an angry message about a mistake you did not make. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reply calmly, clarify the facts, and focus on resolving the issue$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the frustration and ask to discuss the facts$qb$, $qb$Defend yourself immediately and explain who caused the mistake$qb$, $qb$Forward the message to your manager without replying$qb$, $qb$Send an equally strong message so your position is clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You disagree with a teammate about who was responsible for an error. What is the best way to move forward?$qb$, $qb$Check the facts, fix the immediate issue, and clarify responsibility without personal blame$qb$, $qb$Review what each person was expected to do$qb$, $qb$Explain why you believe the teammate was responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide who was at fault$qb$, $qb$Stop cooperating until the issue is settled$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A discussion about a missed task turns into comments about a person's attitude. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Move the discussion back to the task, facts, impact, and next action$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to describe what happened without personal comments$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and ask the manager to handle it$qb$, $qb$Defend the person being criticized$qb$, $qb$Allow the comments so people can express frustration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate criticizes your work in a group message and you disagree with the criticism. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the concern, check the facts, and discuss the issue without attacking the person$qb$, $qb$Ask for the specific example behind the criticism$qb$, $qb$Reply with evidence showing why they are wrong$qb$, $qb$Move the disagreement to a larger group$qb$, $qb$Ignore the message and stop cooperating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker says you caused a delay, but the records show several factors contributed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review the facts together, acknowledge your part, and agree what should change$qb$, $qb$Explain your part and ask them to review the other factors$qb$, $qb$Show the records to prove you were not mainly responsible$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to defend you$qb$, $qb$Blame the other factors instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree about a task and both keep repeating their positions. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Identify the shared outcome and compare the reasons behind each option$qb$, $qb$Summarize both positions and ask what each person needs$qb$, $qb$Ask them to vote on the two options$qb$, $qb$Choose the view of the more experienced teammate$qb$, $qb$End the discussion without a decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the recommended first step when resolving a resource allocation disagreement between two team members?$qb$, $qb$Facilitating a private alignment conversation to re-establish shared project goals and co-create clear task ownership.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning conflicting resources personally to eliminate immediate operational friction and protect project schedules.$qb$, $qb$Advising both team members to resolve their differences professionally before the upcoming project deadline.$qb$, $qb$Escalating the conflict to department managers so task responsibilities can be officially dictated.$qb$, $qb$Allocating contested resources strictly based on employee team seniority to settle the debate rapidly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two vice presidents have turned a resource dispute into personal criticism, and their teams are copying the hostility. What intervention is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Stop personal escalation, define the enterprise decision required, surface interests and evidence, and agree both the resource choice and behavioral expectations.$qb$, $qb$Meet them separately and decide whose resource case is stronger.$qb$, $qb$Ask their common executive to impose a resource split.$qb$, $qb$Move the resources temporarily to a neutral function.$qb$, $qb$Allow the two leaders to resolve the issue themselves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two executives fight over whether to compensate a failed strategic account, with one fearing precedent. What should their leader do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the urgent customer decision from policy precedent, establish shared criteria, and resolve both without personalizing ownership.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two senior leaders agree a portfolio must shrink but each insists the other's projects should be cut. What should their sponsor do?$qb$, $qb$Establish enterprise-level criteria and constraints, compare projects against them transparently, and address incentives that reward leaders for protecting local portfolios.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$At first, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two executive teams blame each other for a customer failure, but recovery requires both within hours. What should the accountable leader do first?$qb$, $qb$Stabilize the customer outcome with clear joint roles, establish a shared factual record, and defer attribution until it will not obstruct recovery.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about how to complete a shared task and the discussion is becoming personal. The disagreement is now affecting delivery and trust. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Refocus them on the shared result, facts, and choices available$qb$, $qb$Ask each person to explain their preferred approach calmly$qb$, $qb$Choose the approach that seems faster so the argument ends$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop discussing it and continue separately$qb$, $qb$Support the colleague whose approach you prefer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-006$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about a failed handoff and each has evidence supporting part of their position. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Separate agreed facts from disputed points, identify the process gap, and agree how the next handoff will work$qb$, $qb$Let each person present their evidence and summarize the areas of agreement$qb$, $qb$Decide which person's evidence is stronger and close the discussion$qb$, $qb$Ask their managers to determine responsibility$qb$, $qb$Avoid assigning any responsibility so the relationship stays positive$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate in another time zone reads 'end of day' differently from you. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Use a specific date, time, and time zone in future requests.$qb$, $qb$Clarify the exact time for this request and use clearer timing next time.$qb$, $qb$Keep saying 'end of day' but add your city name.$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to work out what you probably meant.$qb$, $qb$Continue using the phrase because it is common at work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A remote colleague uses a phrase you do not understand. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask politely what the phrase means in this context$qb$, $qb$Repeat the phrase back and ask whether you understood correctly$qb$, $qb$Guess the meaning from the rest of the message$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate what they think it means$qb$, $qb$Ignore the phrase and continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to explain a local abbreviation they have not seen before. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the full term and what it means in this work context$qb$, $qb$Give the full form and a short example$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone learns it after some time$qb$, $qb$Send them an old message that uses it$qb$, $qb$Tell them to search for it online$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate misunderstands a message because the word 'soon' means different things to different people. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Replace 'soon' with a specific date or time$qb$, $qb$Explain what you meant by 'soon' in a follow-up$qb$, $qb$Use 'as soon as possible' next time$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to check whenever timing is unclear$qb$, $qb$Keep using 'soon' because it is common English$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global team message uses several local jokes that some colleagues do not understand. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Replace the jokes with clear language that everyone can understand$qb$, $qb$Keep one joke but explain its meaning$qb$, $qb$Send separate explanations to colleagues who ask$qb$, $qb$Assume people will understand from context$qb$, $qb$Use more local jokes so the message feels friendly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says your email sounds too direct in their work culture. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what wording felt too direct and adjust the message while keeping the action clear$qb$, $qb$Thank them and soften the wording where appropriate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you always write directly$qb$, $qb$Use very indirect language in all future emails$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment because the meaning was clear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most effective method to ensure clear task execution when working with international remote collaborators?$qb$, $qb$Standardizing written communications with plain language, bulleted action items, and explicit verification check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Relying on frequent informal video calls to discuss task expectations and resolve misunderstandings verbally.$qb$, $qb$Sending automated task reminders at higher frequencies to ensure project deadlines remain top of mind.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning complex analytical tasks to local team members while leaving routine steps with global collaborators.$qb$, $qb$Using standard region-specific idioms and assuming collaborators will seek clarification if context is unclear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regional team says a global instruction is clear in English but culturally reads as accusatory in their market. The factual requirement must not change. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Preserve the required meaning while adapting tone and examples locally, then validate that the translated version cannot be misread.$qb$, $qb$Use a professional translation service and keep the original wording as closely as possible.$qb$, $qb$Let the region rewrite the instruction entirely because they understand the local culture better.$qb$, $qb$Keep the global wording unchanged so every country receives identical language.$qb$, $qb$Remove the sensitive sentence from the regional version to avoid offense.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A globally valid policy sounds accusatory when translated directly into one region's language and may suppress reporting. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Preserve intent and controls while adapting wording and examples with local expertise, then verify equivalent meaning.$qb$, $qb$Use the direct translation because wording must be consistent.$qb$, $qb$Let the regional team rewrite it without central constraints.$qb$, $qb$Add a disclaimer explaining the wording may sound unusually strong.$qb$, $qb$Keep only the English version authoritative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global employee survey phrase becomes a serious accusation when translated in several countries, depressing response rates. What is the best correction?$qb$, $qb$Preserve intended meaning, use culturally validated wording, and test equivalence before comparing results across regions.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global succession process values 'executive presence,' but regional leaders interpret the phrase very differently. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the vague label with observable, role-relevant behaviors and evidence, then calibrate assessors across cultures without prescribing one interpersonal style.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$In this case, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague writes in simple English and asks you to review their idea. The wording may be reused by people in different countries. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Focus on the idea first and clarify any wording that affects meaning$qb$, $qb$Respond to the idea and suggest clearer wording where needed$qb$, $qb$Correct their grammar before discussing the idea$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the whole message in your preferred style$qb$, $qb$Treat unclear grammar as evidence that the idea is weak$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teams in different countries interpret the phrase 'urgent' differently, causing repeated confusion. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Agree clear response-time definitions and use specific deadlines for important requests$qb$, $qb$Use exact dates and times whenever a request is important$qb$, $qb$Explain how your team normally uses the word 'urgent'$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to adapt to the other team's style$qb$, $qb$Keep using the term and resolve misunderstandings when they occur$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global project uses technical shorthand that is clear to specialists but confusing to several business users. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Use necessary terms consistently, explain them once in plain language, and keep key actions understandable to all readers$qb$, $qb$Add a short glossary and use simpler wording in important messages$qb$, $qb$Continue using the shorthand and answer questions when they arise$qb$, $qb$Replace all technical terms even when precision is lost$qb$, $qb$Send separate technical and non-technical versions without common wording$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems confused by an idiom you used in a message. The message will be reused with colleagues from different backgrounds. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the point in simple, direct language$qb$, $qb$Explain what the idiom means in this case$qb$, $qb$Use a different idiom that may be easier$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to explain it$qb$, $qb$Continue using the same wording$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You and a colleague both need the same meeting room at the same time. What is the best way to resolve it?$qb$, $qb$Discuss which need is more time-sensitive and find a workable split$qb$, $qb$Check whether one meeting can move by a short time$qb$, $qb$Book the room first and let the colleague adjust$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide who gets the room$qb$, $qb$Cancel your meeting without discussing alternatives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You and a colleague both need the same resource at the same time. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Compare urgency and impact, then agree a workable timing or sharing arrangement.$qb$, $qb$Ask whether either person's use can move by a short time.$qb$, $qb$Take the resource first because you booked it earlier.$qb$, $qb$Ask a manager to decide without discussing options together.$qb$, $qb$Cancel your own need immediately without exploring alternatives.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to take an extra task, but your current deadline is at risk. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain your workload and agree which task should take priority$qb$, $qb$Offer to help with part of the task after your deadline$qb$, $qb$Accept the task and try to manage both$qb$, $qb$Refuse immediately because your schedule is full$qb$, $qb$Accept it but give it very little attention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate needs your help this afternoon, but you have only 30 minutes free. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree what can realistically be achieved in 30 minutes and focus on that$qb$, $qb$Offer the 30 minutes and ask what is most urgent$qb$, $qb$Help until your next task becomes urgent$qb$, $qb$Say you are too busy to help$qb$, $qb$Promise more time than you have$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues need your help at the same time and both requests matter. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify urgency and impact with both, then agree the order and timing$qb$, $qb$Tell both how much time you have and ask which can wait$qb$, $qb$Help the person who asked first$qb$, $qb$Choose the easier request so one is completed quickly$qb$, $qb$Promise both immediate help$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker asks for your work by noon, but you can reliably deliver it at 2 PM. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the constraint and agree whether 2 PM works or the scope should change$qb$, $qb$Offer 2 PM and ask what part is needed most urgently$qb$, $qb$Promise noon and try to finish faster$qb$, $qb$Refuse the request because noon is impossible$qb$, $qb$Say 'I'll try' without giving a realistic time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague wants your draft today, but you need until tomorrow to check important figures. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the quality risk and agree whether they need an unchecked draft today or a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Offer an early draft today and a checked version tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Send the draft today without explaining the unchecked figures$qb$, $qb$Refuse to share anything until tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Promise a checked version today even though it is unrealistic$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$When negotiating project scope adjustments with a client under tight budget limits, what represents the most effective approach?$qb$, $qb$Evaluating core underlying priorities to rebalance deliverable scope while protecting project margin boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Offering a standard percentage price reduction immediately to demonstrate goodwill and secure contract agreement.$qb$, $qb$Reiterating standard rate card policies firmly and advising the client that adjustments are unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Escalating the negotiation directly to executive leadership to determine if margin exceptions can be granted.$qb$, $qb$Accepting all requested scope additions at current pricing to maintain positive client sentiment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are renegotiating a strategic contract. The supplier can lower unit price, improve service levels, or absorb more demand risk, but not all three. What should guide your trade-off?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the combination that creates the best total value against business objectives, risk exposure, and likely future demand.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lowest unit price because it is the easiest benefit to quantify.$qb$, $qb$Split the concessions evenly across price, service, and risk so both sides compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier which concession is least costly for them and accept that one.$qb$, $qb$Delay the negotiation until you can secure all three concessions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two business units both want control of a shared platform because future revenue allocation depends on ownership. What is the best negotiating basis?$qb$, $qb$Separate governance, economics and operating responsibilities, identify shared interests, and align decision rights with enterprise value and risk.$qb$, $qb$Split ownership equally so neither side dominates.$qb$, $qb$Give control to the unit contributing most current revenue.$qb$, $qb$Ask the executive committee to select an owner first.$qb$, $qb$Let both units control the platform independently in their markets.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A potential alliance partner wants broad intellectual-property rights in return for access to a market you cannot enter alone. What is the strongest negotiation approach?$qb$, $qb$Separate access, ownership, fields of use, duration and exit rights, then construct exchanges that protect strategic options while giving the partner sufficient value.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-005$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global succession process strongly favors executives whose careers match the current board's traditional path, although future strategy requires different experiences. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Define future-role capabilities and evidence afresh, recognize alternative career pathways, calibrate assessors and stop treating resemblance to incumbents as proof of readiness.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You can meet a requested deadline only by reducing the amount of work included. The trade-off will affect another deadline already agreed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the deadline and scope together and agree the best trade-off$qb$, $qb$Offer a smaller scope that can be delivered on time$qb$, $qb$Accept the deadline and decide later what can be completed$qb$, $qb$Reject the deadline because the full scope cannot fit$qb$, $qb$Promise the full scope and hope extra effort is enough$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder wants an earlier deadline without reducing scope, while your team has a fixed capacity. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify what matters most, make the time-scope-capacity trade-offs visible, and agree a realistic option$qb$, $qb$Offer an earlier delivery for the highest-priority part and the rest later$qb$, $qb$Accept the deadline and ask the team to find a way$qb$, $qb$Reject the request because capacity is fixed$qb$, $qb$Agree to the date but quietly reduce lower-priority work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is unsure about using a new shared tracker. Which approach is most likely to gain support?$qb$, $qb$Show how it will reduce missed updates for the team$qb$, $qb$Explain its main features and ask them to try it$qb$, $qb$Tell them that most people already use it$qb$, $qb$Say that the manager prefers the new tracker$qb$, $qb$Keep using it yourself and hope they follow$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is reluctant to use a shared checklist. What is the best way to gain support?$qb$, $qb$Show a recent problem the checklist would prevent and invite them to try it on the next task.$qb$, $qb$Explain how the checklist works and ask them to use it for a week.$qb$, $qb$Tell them most people already use the checklist.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to tell them to use it.$qb$, $qb$Keep using it yourself and hope they follow later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want your team to record one extra field because it reduces follow-up questions. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the repeated follow-up problem, explain how the field prevents it, and ask the team to test it.$qb$, $qb$Explain the new field and ask the team to use it.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the field is good practice.$qb$, $qb$Add the field without discussing why it is needed.$qb$, $qb$Record it only in your own work and leave others unchanged.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to adopt a checklist that has reduced errors in your work. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the error reduction and invite them to try it on one task$qb$, $qb$Explain how the checklist works and share a copy$qb$, $qb$Tell them the checklist is now your preferred method$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to recommend the checklist to them$qb$, $qb$Keep the checklist to yourself because adoption is their choice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to start using a shared calendar for deadlines. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show how missed dates have caused problems and how the calendar can prevent them$qb$, $qb$Explain how the shared calendar works and suggest a short trial$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone should use the same calendar$qb$, $qb$Add their deadlines to the calendar yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to make calendar use compulsory$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a colleague to adopt a short review step before sending customer emails. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show an example of an avoidable error and how the review step would catch it$qb$, $qb$Explain the review step and ask them to try it for a week$qb$, $qb$Tell them careful people always review emails$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to add the step to their goals$qb$, $qb$Review their emails yourself instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a teammate to use a simple naming rule because files are often hard to find. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the time lost finding files and demonstrate how the naming rule makes retrieval easier$qb$, $qb$Explain the naming rule and ask them to try it on new files$qb$, $qb$Tell them organized teams use naming rules$qb$, $qb$Rename their files yourself$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to enforce the rule$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Country leaders must change practices that help them hit local targets but undermine an enterprise transformation. How should you build support?$qb$, $qb$Expose the incentive conflict, connect the change to shared outcomes, co-design trade-offs, and align measures with the new behavior.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need several autonomous divisions to share scarce specialists with an enterprise program, although each division will miss local targets by doing so. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Make the enterprise value and local cost explicit, reset conflicting targets or incentives, and secure commitments through a transparent allocation and governance process.$qb$, $qb$At first, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$In this case, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-004$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic partner offers exceptional economics in return for a perpetual right to participate in any future transaction in your sector. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Value the future participation right independently, narrow scope, duration and triggering conditions, and trade it only if its long-term option cost is justified by the current deal.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You want a colleague to change a small work habit that causes repeated errors. The colleague has discretion and must be persuaded rather than instructed. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the error pattern, explain the benefit of the change, and ask them to try it$qb$, $qb$Explain the new habit and give an example of how to use it$qb$, $qb$Tell them the current habit is causing problems$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior colleague to tell them to change$qb$, $qb$Wait until another error happens before raising it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need another team to change a handoff practice that saves them time but creates repeated rework for your team. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Show the end-to-end impact, understand their constraint, and propose a change that improves the handoff for both teams$qb$, $qb$Share evidence of the rework and ask them to adjust the handoff$qb$, $qb$Explain how much extra work their current practice creates for your team$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to both managers so the change is enforced$qb$, $qb$Create a workaround within your team and stop asking them to change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Another team is hesitant to adopt your proposed handoff change because they expect extra effort. Which approach is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge their concern, show the shared benefit with evidence, and design the change with them so the effort is practical$qb$, $qb$Show the evidence and ask them to trial the change for a short period$qb$, $qb$Explain that the current handoff creates avoidable work for your team$qb$, $qb$Escalate the change because cross-team consistency is important$qb$, $qb$Implement a workaround without changing their handoff$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a sync meeting, an upset cross-departmental partner speaks rapidly and emotionally about recurring process delays caused by your team. How do you respond during the conversation?$qb$, $qb$Listen attentively without interrupting, summarize their key pain points in your own words to confirm understanding, and ask clarifying questions on specific bottlenecks.$qb$, $qb$Let them speak until they finish, then immediately explain the operational constraints your team faces to clarify why the delays occurred.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge their frustration briefly and agree on the spot to modify your team's workflow to relieve the immediate pressure.$qb$, $qb$Politely interrupt the conversation to ask that they focus strictly on specific data points rather than general emotional feedback.$qb$, $qb$Note down their points silently during the call, then email your supervisor after the meeting to handle the inter-departmental conflict.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says your team's handover is 'always incomplete' and gives two recent examples, but one example appears unrelated. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Let them finish, summarize the pattern you heard, separate the two examples, and ask which missing information caused the greatest impact.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the frustration and ask them to send a list of everything they want included in future handovers.$qb$, $qb$Explain why one of the examples was not actually your team's responsibility.$qb$, $qb$Agree that handovers need improvement and promise to remind the team to be more careful.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and ask both managers to review the handover process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1104$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells you a customer 'keeps changing their mind.' Their examples involve different people from the customer's team. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask who requested each change, what decision authority they had, and what was previously agreed, then summarize whether the problem is changing preference or unclear customer governance.$qb$, $qb$Agree that the customer is inconsistent and suggest stricter change control.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to stop accepting changes from anyone except the most senior customer.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to put every request in writing before discussing the examples.$qb$, $qb$Assume the different people represent one customer view.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1288$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate says a client 'ignored our advice.' When you ask what happened, you learn the client chose another option after raising a concern the team did not answer. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what concern remained unanswered, listen to the client's reasoning as reported, and distinguish deliberate rejection of advice from a decision made with an unresolved information gap.$qb$, $qb$Agree that the client ignored expert advice.$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate to repeat the recommendation more firmly.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client why they do not trust the team.$qb$, $qb$Stop advising the client if they do not follow recommendations.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-138$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate tells you that your frequent check-ins are making it harder for them to finish a time-sensitive task. You believe the check-ins are necessary because the work is important. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain when the check-ins are disruptive, reflect back what you heard, then agree a lighter update rhythm that still gives enough visibility.$qb$, $qb$Let them explain, then reduce the number of check-ins while keeping the same reporting format.$qb$, $qb$Explain why the task is important and ask them to be patient with the check-ins until it is finished.$qb$, $qb$Stop checking in and wait until the deadline so they can work without interruption.$qb$, $qb$Tell them that regular reporting is part of professional work and continue as before.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1380$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a customer is unhappy with the service. Their only evidence is that the customer asked several detailed questions after delivery. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the customer actually said and how they responded to the answers, distinguishing curiosity or due diligence from evidence of dissatisfaction before drawing a conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Assume the questions show dissatisfaction and prepare a recovery plan.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague detailed questions usually mean the customer is engaged.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer directly whether they are unhappy before reviewing the interaction.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the concern because no complaint was made.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1518$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a customer 'keeps asking the same question.' You notice the team's answers have used different terminology each time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the earlier answers, ask what remains unclear to the customer, and provide one consistent explanation using terms the customer understands.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the question has already been answered.$qb$, $qb$Send all previous answers together.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior colleague to answer so the customer accepts it.$qb$, $qb$Stop responding until the customer asks a different question.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1610$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a customer rejected a proposal because 'they only care about price.' The customer's message also mentions implementation disruption. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review both stated concerns and clarify their relative importance with the customer before deciding what to change.$qb$, $qb$Offer a lower price first because it is the clearest objection.$qb$, $qb$Add more implementation detail without checking what matters most.$qb$, $qb$Assume disruption is only a negotiating tactic.$qb$, $qb$Resend the same proposal.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-184$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a recurring meeting is wasting their time. They sound frustrated, but you know the meeting has an important purpose. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask which parts feel wasteful, listen without defending the meeting, summarize their concerns, and then discuss how the purpose could be achieved with less wasted time.$qb$, $qb$Let them explain, then describe why the meeting matters and ask what they would shorten.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the meeting length immediately because their frustration suggests it is too long.$qb$, $qb$Tell them attendance is required because the meeting serves a wider purpose.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send suggestions later so you can return to your work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-230$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate says your instructions for a recurring task are difficult to follow. You have used the same instructions successfully with several other people. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to walk you through where they lose clarity, listen without comparing them with others, summarize the difficulty, and agree what clarification would make the task easier to perform.$qb$, $qb$Let them explain the problem, then show them how other teammates interpret the instructions.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to mark the unclear sentences in writing so you can revise them later.$qb$, $qb$Explain that others have followed the instructions successfully and suggest they try once more.$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate to coach them instead of changing instructions that already work for most people.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2806$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they are being excluded from decisions. When you ask for examples, they begin describing several small incidents from different meetings. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Let them explain the examples, look for the common pattern, summarize what you think is happening, and confirm it before discussing remedies.$qb$, $qb$Stop after the first clear example and suggest a solution.$qb$, $qb$Explain that different meetings naturally involve different people.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to write all incidents down before you discuss the issue.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to speak directly to the people they believe excluded them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-322$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague complains that another team 'never responds on time.' You know of at least one recent case where that team responded quickly. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask for specific examples and the impact, listen for the underlying pattern, summarize what you heard, and avoid challenging the generalization until you understand the concern.$qb$, $qb$Let them explain, then point out the recent case where the other team responded quickly.$qb$, $qb$Tell them 'never' is inaccurate and ask them to use more precise language.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team for their side before listening further.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the complaint because your own experience with the other team has been different.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-368$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they disagree with a decision and begins explaining why, but you were part of making the decision and feel responsible for defending it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Listen to their reasoning without defending the decision, ask questions about the impact they see, summarize the concern, and only then explain or reconsider the decision.$qb$, $qb$Let them finish and then explain the reasons the decision was made.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to focus only on new facts because the decision has already been discussed.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise the disagreement with someone who was not involved in the decision.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion because reopening agreed decisions can undermine execution.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-414$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer explains a problem in a long story containing details that seem unrelated. You are under time pressure and think you already know the likely cause. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Let the customer reach the main point, ask focused questions to separate relevant details, summarize the problem you understood, and confirm it before proposing a solution.$qb$, $qb$Interrupt politely and ask only the questions needed to test your likely cause.$qb$, $qb$Let the customer finish but ignore details that do not fit your initial diagnosis.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send the story by email so you can review it later.$qb$, $qb$Give the likely solution immediately and ask them to return if it does not work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-460$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells you that a change you introduced has made their work harder. Their first example seems minor, and you are tempted to explain why the change is necessary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to describe the impact and any other examples, reflect back the pattern you hear, and only then discuss the purpose of the change or possible adjustments.$qb$, $qb$Let them finish the first example and then explain why the change was introduced.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to document all problems before you discuss whether the change should be adjusted.$qb$, $qb$Tell them some inconvenience is expected whenever a process changes.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion because one minor example is not enough evidence to reconsider the change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-506$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they are unhappy with how work is allocated, but their first comments sound like a complaint about one difficult task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the broader pattern, listen for examples and impact, summarize what they believe is unfair, and confirm the issue before discussing solutions.$qb$, $qb$Discuss the difficult task first and see whether resolving it addresses their concern.$qb$, $qb$Explain how work allocation decisions are made and ask whether that changes their view.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to submit a list of tasks they consider unfairly allocated.$qb$, $qb$Tell them every employee occasionally receives difficult work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-552$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate says your feedback on their work is too detailed and makes them unsure what matters most. You believe all the points are valid. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask which parts of the feedback feel hard to prioritize, listen to how they use it, summarize the issue, and agree a clearer way to distinguish critical changes from optional improvements.$qb$, $qb$Explain that all the feedback is valid but offer to mark the most important points next time.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the amount of feedback you give even if some useful points are omitted.$qb$, $qb$Tell them professional growth requires handling detailed feedback.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to decide for themselves which feedback matters most.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-644$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a shared tool is 'impossible to use.' You know many people use it successfully. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to show where they get stuck, listen to the steps and impact, summarize the actual difficulty, and only then discuss whether the issue is training, design, access, or something else.$qb$, $qb$Tell them many colleagues use the tool successfully and ask what they are doing differently.$qb$, $qb$Offer to show them the correct way to use the tool.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to submit a support ticket with screenshots.$qb$, $qb$Conclude that they need more training because the tool works for others.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-690$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a recent decision has created 'extra work everywhere.' You think the statement is exaggerated. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to walk through where the extra work appears, listen for repeated causes, summarize the actual impact, and only then test whether the effect is as broad as they first described.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the statement sounds exaggerated and ask for evidence.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to list every additional task in writing before discussing it.$qb$, $qb$Explain the benefits of the decision so they understand why some extra work is acceptable.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the complaint because broad statements are rarely accurate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-736$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a new handoff is causing delays, but they struggle to explain exactly where the delay occurs. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to walk through a recent handoff step by step, listen for waiting points, summarize what you heard, and confirm the suspected delay point before proposing changes.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to track the next few handoffs and return with clearer evidence.$qb$, $qb$Suggest the delay is probably at the approval step because that is usually slow.$qb$, $qb$Ask the receiving team whether they think the handoff is delayed.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the issue is too vague to act on.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-828$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they do not feel included in project decisions, although they attend every meeting. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for examples of when they felt excluded, listen for how decisions are actually being made, summarize the pattern, and explore whether timing, influence, information, or meeting dynamics are the issue.$qb$, $qb$Point out that they attend every meeting and ask what more inclusion would mean.$qb$, $qb$Invite them to speak first at the next meeting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager whether the colleague participates enough.$qb$, $qb$Tell them inclusion also depends on taking initiative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-874$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a customer meeting went badly but mostly describes what they themselves said. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what the customer said, questioned, or did at key moments, listen for evidence of their concerns, and summarize what appears to have made the meeting difficult.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague which parts of their message probably caused the problem.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send the recording instead of discussing it.$qb$, $qb$Explain how you would have handled the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Conclude that they need stronger presentation skills.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-920$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An employee says a new rota is not workable but becomes quiet when asked for details in the group.$qb$, $qb$Offer a private way to explain the concern, listen for specific constraints, and confirm your understanding before judging the rota.$qb$, $qb$Ask again in front of everyone.$qb$, $qb$Assume it is personal preference.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager what the problem is.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rota unless they file a complaint.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-001$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client requests an immediate update on a project deliverable that is currently undergoing quality review. The review will take two more hours, but delaying your response could cause client anxiety. How do you handle this communication?$qb$, $qb$Send a prompt email stating the current review stage, the specific 2-hour completion window, and a summary of validated components completed so far.$qb$, $qb$Reply immediately reassuring the client that everything is on track and that you will send the complete file as soon as the review finishes later today.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the quality review is 100% finished before sending a response so you can deliver the complete final document in a single email.$qb$, $qb$Send the unverified draft immediately to satisfy the client's request, noting that a verified version will follow after quality checks.$qb$, $qb$Forward the client's email directly to the quality review lead, asking them to send an update directly to the client.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-093$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior colleague asks you for a 'quick status' before a steering meeting. The project is broadly on track, but one dependency could delay a milestone if it is not resolved this week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give a concise status with current progress, the specific dependency, its possible effect, the owner, and the decision or action needed before the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Say the project is on track and mention that one small dependency is being watched.$qb$, $qb$Send the full project tracker so the senior colleague can review all details.$qb$, $qb$Wait until you know whether the dependency actually causes a delay.$qb$, $qb$Ask the dependency owner to brief the senior colleague directly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1103$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior colleague asks you to send a project update immediately. One workstream has not reported today, but yesterday it was on schedule. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send the confirmed status, state that the one workstream has not yet refreshed its position, explain whether that creates any material uncertainty, and follow up with a time for the complete update.$qb$, $qb$Use yesterday's status for the missing workstream and send the update as fully current.$qb$, $qb$Wait for every workstream before sending anything.$qb$, $qb$Mark the missing workstream as delayed because it has not reported.$qb$, $qb$Ask the senior colleague to contact the workstream directly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1149$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks whether an issue is fully resolved. The immediate fault is fixed, but monitoring has only run for one hour and similar faults previously returned after several hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Say the fault is fixed but resolution is still being verified, explain the monitoring period and next update point, and avoid declaring permanent resolution too early.$qb$, $qb$Say the issue is resolved because the system is currently working.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the issue is probably resolved but may return.$qb$, $qb$Wait until monitoring is complete and give no update now.$qb$, $qb$Ask the technical team to decide what to tell the customer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1195$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a simple red/amber/green status. Most work is green, but a small unresolved issue could block final acceptance. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Report the overall status with the acceptance risk explicitly called out, explain its likelihood and next resolution point, and avoid letting a single color hide a material condition.$qb$, $qb$Mark green because most work is on track.$qb$, $qb$Mark amber without explaining the specific issue.$qb$, $qb$Give separate colors for every task instead of answering the request.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the issue is resolved before providing a status.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1241$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks whether a supplier delay will affect the final deadline. The delayed item has three days of schedule buffer, but another dependency could use that buffer later. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that the final date has not moved, quantify the buffer now consumed, identify the remaining dependency risk, and state when the deadline will be reassessed.$qb$, $qb$Say the final deadline is unchanged because three days of buffer remain.$qb$, $qb$Say the deadline is now three days late because the supplier is late.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder not to worry unless another dependency slips.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the buffer is fully used before mentioning the delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1287$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department head asks whether a customer complaint is an isolated case. You have confirmed one similar case, but the monthly trend report is not ready. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Say there is at least one similar confirmed case, explain that the broader frequency is still being checked, and give a time for the trend view rather than calling it isolated or widespread.$qb$, $qb$Call it isolated because only two cases are currently known.$qb$, $qb$Say it is a recurring problem because a second case exists.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the monthly report and provide no current view.$qb$, $qb$Ask the department head to decide how serious two cases are.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-137$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$After a process change, two departments are using different versions of the same instruction. People are beginning to complete the work differently. You are asked to clarify the position. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the current approved instruction, send both departments one concise clarification showing what changed, who is affected, and from when, and ask team leads to correct any conflicting local guidance.$qb$, $qb$Send the latest instruction to both departments and ask them to use that version from now on.$qb$, $qb$Tell the two team leads that their teams are working differently and ask them to align the details between themselves.$qb$, $qb$Explain the change separately to the people who contact you, without issuing a common clarification.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next scheduled process meeting because changing instructions again may create more confusion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1379$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A steering-group member asks why a task shows 80% complete for the third week. Most routine work is done, but the remaining work is a difficult approval. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what the 80% represents, identify the approval as the remaining critical work, and report progress using milestones or remaining effort rather than repeating a misleading percentage.$qb$, $qb$Keep reporting 80% until the approval is obtained.$qb$, $qb$Increase the percentage slightly each week to show progress.$qb$, $qb$Report the task as nearly complete because most activities are finished.$qb$, $qb$Remove the percentage and provide no alternative status.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1425$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A leader asks whether a corrective action is working. The latest week is better, but normal performance varies considerably week to week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the better week in context of normal variation, define what sustained improvement would look like, and continue monitoring before calling the action proven.$qb$, $qb$Say the action is working because the latest week improved.$qb$, $qb$Say it is not working until several months pass.$qb$, $qb$Average the last two weeks and call that the trend.$qb$, $qb$Stop reporting until the pattern is obvious.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1471$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks whether a recurring defect has been eliminated. No defect appeared in the last two batches, but only a small number of units were produced. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that the latest batches are encouraging but the sample is still limited, state what monitoring will confirm sustained improvement, and avoid declaring the defect eliminated yet.$qb$, $qb$Say the defect is eliminated because two batches were clean.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing has improved until full production resumes.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the defect is probably gone.$qb$, $qb$Wait for a failure before discussing the issue again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1517$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior stakeholder asks whether a new supplier is performing better than the old one. Delivery speed is better, but only half as many orders have been placed so far. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the faster delivery result together with the smaller sample and any other comparable measures, and state when there will be enough evidence for a firmer comparison.$qb$, $qb$Say the new supplier is better because deliveries are faster.$qb$, $qb$Say no comparison is possible until order volumes are identical.$qb$, $qb$Compare only the fastest delivery from each supplier.$qb$, $qb$Ask the stakeholder which supplier they prefer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1563$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks whether a revised process is faster. The last five cases were faster, but they were all simple cases. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Report the improvement for simple cases, explain that complex-case performance is not yet tested, and state how you will compare the full case mix before claiming overall improvement.$qb$, $qb$Say the revised process is faster because all five cases improved.$qb$, $qb$Say there is no improvement until complex cases are tested.$qb$, $qb$Average the five cases and use that as the new standard time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to decide whether five cases are enough.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1609$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client asks whether a cost overrun will continue next month. The overrun came from a one-time repair, while normal monthly costs remain within plan. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the one-time repair from recurring cost, explain the current run rate, and state any remaining uncertainty before giving the next-month outlook.$qb$, $qb$Say next month should be within plan because the repair is finished.$qb$, $qb$Report only that this month is over budget.$qb$, $qb$Spread the repair across future months to smooth the trend.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to answer instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-229$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer-facing team has misunderstood one sentence in your project update and is now telling customers that a feature will be available a week earlier than planned. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the customer-facing team immediately, send a revised update with the date stated unambiguously, and identify any customers who may already have received the wrong expectation.$qb$, $qb$Send the team a corrected date and ask them to use it in future conversations.$qb$, $qb$Update the project document and assume the customer-facing team will check it before their next conversations.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to correct the team because the misunderstanding occurred outside your department.$qb$, $qb$Wait until a customer raises the discrepancy before changing the message.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2759$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A steering pack shows all workstreams green, but one workstream's status has not been updated for three weeks and its owner is on leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the overall view provisional, obtain current evidence or an accountable substitute update for that workstream, and make the stale status visible rather than treating green as current.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2805$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks for a final number before a meeting. Your analysis is complete, but a late data correction from another team has not yet been validated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the current result is provisional, explain the pending correction and its possible effect, validate it urgently, and provide a confirmed number at an agreed time.$qb$, $qb$Give the current number and update it later if necessary.$qb$, $qb$Use the corrected number immediately because it came from the data-owning team.$qb$, $qb$Delay the answer without explaining what is pending.$qb$, $qb$Give both numbers and ask the customer which one they want to use.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-321$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks whether a delayed task will affect Friday's delivery. You know it might, but the dependency owner will confirm by 3 p.m. What do you say?$qb$, $qb$Explain that Friday is currently at risk rather than confirmed late, state the dependency causing the uncertainty, and commit to a firm update after the 3 p.m. confirmation.$qb$, $qb$Say Friday may be affected and that you will know more later today.$qb$, $qb$Say the team is still aiming for Friday and avoid mentioning the risk until it is confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the dependency owner to answer the manager because they control the uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Say Friday remains the delivery date because no formal delay has been confirmed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-367$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need to tell two teams that a planned change is cancelled for now. One team has already begun preparation and the other has not. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send both teams the cancellation and reason at the same time, explicitly tell the first team what preparation should stop or preserve, and confirm the next decision point.$qb$, $qb$Tell both teams the change is cancelled and that more information will follow.$qb$, $qb$Contact only the team that has started preparation because the other team has not acted yet.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to communicate the cancellation because it affects more than one team.$qb$, $qb$Wait until a new date is known so you can communicate the cancellation and replacement plan together.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-459$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior leader asks for the cause of a service problem while the investigation is still underway. You know what happened immediately before the failure, but not whether it caused it. What do you say?$qb$, $qb$State the confirmed sequence, distinguish it from the still-unproven cause, explain what is being checked, and give the next investigation update time.$qb$, $qb$Describe the event that happened immediately before the failure as the likely cause and note that investigation continues.$qb$, $qb$Say the cause is unknown and avoid giving any other detail until the investigation ends.$qb$, $qb$Ask the technical team to answer because they are conducting the investigation.$qb$, $qb$Give the most plausible cause so the leader has something useful to communicate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-505$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project risk has become less likely after new information, but it has not disappeared. Several stakeholders still believe the risk is as serious as it was last week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send a concise update explaining what changed, how the likelihood or impact has reduced, what risk remains, and when it will be reviewed again.$qb$, $qb$Tell stakeholders that the risk is now much lower and no longer needs close attention.$qb$, $qb$Update the risk register and wait for stakeholders to notice the revised rating.$qb$, $qb$Ask the risk owner to explain the change because they supplied the new information.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing until the risk is either fully removed or becomes serious again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-551$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A cross-team dependency is delayed by two days, but your own final deadline may still be met if later work goes smoothly. A stakeholder asks whether the deadline has moved. What do you say?$qb$, $qb$Explain that the final deadline has not yet moved, state that the dependency delay has reduced the remaining buffer, and give the point at which you will confirm whether recovery is still realistic.$qb$, $qb$Say the deadline is unchanged because the team can still recover.$qb$, $qb$Say the deadline is likely to move by two days because the dependency moved by two days.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder to ask the dependency team because they caused the delay.$qb$, $qb$Wait until you know the final outcome before mentioning the reduced buffer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-597$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to confirm whether a customer request is approved. The approval meeting happened, but the written decision has not yet been issued and you heard only part of the discussion. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Say that you cannot yet confirm approval, identify what you do know, and check the authoritative decision source before giving the colleague a definite answer.$qb$, $qb$Say it was probably approved based on the part of the discussion you heard.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to proceed unless someone later says the request was rejected.$qb$, $qb$Ask another attendee informally whether they think it was approved.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing until the written decision arrives, even if the colleague's work is blocked.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-643$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks whether a planned improvement will definitely remove a recurring problem. Testing is encouraging, but the improvement has not yet been used under peak conditions. What do you say?$qb$, $qb$Explain that early testing is positive, state what has and has not yet been proven, and describe the peak-condition validation still required before promising full resolution.$qb$, $qb$Say the improvement should solve the problem based on current testing.$qb$, $qb$Say you cannot comment until every test is complete.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the technical team is confident and leave the detail to them.$qb$, $qb$Promise resolution because showing uncertainty may reduce customer confidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-689$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a progress percentage on work that contains three very different activities. Two are nearly complete, but the hardest activity has barely started. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain progress by the meaningful work components and remaining risk rather than giving a misleading simple percentage, and state what milestone will show genuine overall progress next.$qb$, $qb$Average the completion percentages of the three activities and give that number.$qb$, $qb$Say the work is mostly complete because two of three activities are nearly finished.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder percentages are unreliable and provide no progress estimate.$qb$, $qb$Give the percentage used in the project tracker even if it hides the difficult remaining work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-735$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project update says a task is 'on track,' but you know one assumption behind the date has not been confirmed. A director asks for a simple status. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Say the task is currently on track subject to the unconfirmed assumption, state when that assumption will be resolved, and explain the impact if it fails.$qb$, $qb$Say the task is on track because the current date has not changed.$qb$, $qb$Say the task is at risk without explaining why.$qb$, $qb$Ask the assumption owner to provide the status directly.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the assumption is confirmed before giving any status.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-781$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A weekly report contains a forecast that changed significantly after the report was drafted. The report is due in 20 minutes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Update the forecast and the explanation of its impact, clearly flag the late change, and send the report only after the headline message matches the current evidence.$qb$, $qb$Replace the forecast number but leave the original commentary unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Send the report on time and issue a correction afterward.$qb$, $qb$Remove the forecast section because there is no time to rewrite it.$qb$, $qb$Send the draft because it was accurate when prepared.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-827$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks why a milestone moved. The delay came from three small causes, and none alone explains it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the combined causes, quantify their effect where possible, state what has changed in the recovery plan, and avoid blaming one cause simply because it is easiest to describe.$qb$, $qb$Choose the largest cause and present it as the reason for the delay.$qb$, $qb$List all three causes without explaining their combined impact.$qb$, $qb$Say the milestone moved because of several unforeseen issues and give no detail.$qb$, $qb$Ask each cause owner to explain their own part separately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-965$qb$, $qb$A3-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks you to confirm a delivery date, but two teams have given different estimates. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Find the reason for the difference, communicate the current range and key assumption, and state when the firm date will be confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Choose the later estimate to be safe.$qb$, $qb$Choose the earlier estimate to encourage speed.$qb$, $qb$Give both dates without explaining them.$qb$, $qb$Avoid giving any timing until the teams agree.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-052$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues are arguing over who caused a missed deadline. Both contributed to the problem and the next delivery is due soon. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the discussion to facts, separate contribution from blame, agree the immediate recovery actions, and define a clearer handoff for the next delivery.$qb$, $qb$Ask each colleague to explain their side and decide whose mistake was larger.$qb$, $qb$Tell both colleagues to stop arguing and focus only on the next deadline.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the disagreement to their manager so accountability can be assigned.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing the cause because it may damage the relationship.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-098$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two team members have stopped sharing information after a disagreement. Their work now depends on each other. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Meet with them together, establish the shared work objective, surface the specific issue without personal attacks, and agree practical communication rules for the remaining work.$qb$, $qb$Tell both people they must behave professionally and share information.$qb$, $qb$Route all information through you until the project ends.$qb$, $qb$Move one person to another task.$qb$, $qb$Escalate both employees for unprofessional conduct.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1108$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree over a customer change. One says it was approved in a meeting; the other says the written scope was never updated. Delivery work has started. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the meeting decision, approval authority and written scope, establish the valid current requirement, pause only affected work if necessary, and correct the change-recording process.$qb$, $qb$Follow the written scope because documents always take priority.$qb$, $qb$Follow the meeting decision because it is more recent.$qb$, $qb$Let the two colleagues agree a compromise requirement.$qb$, $qb$Continue delivery until a manager resolves the disagreement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1154$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teams disagree about a recurring defect. One says the specification is unclear; the other says the specification is clear but not followed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review actual defect examples against the specification with both teams, identify whether the gap is wording, execution, or both, and agree the corrective action from evidence.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the specification immediately.$qb$, $qb$Retrain the team that performs the work.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to decide which team is correct.$qb$, $qb$Split responsibility equally without investigating.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1292$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teams disagree about a data error. One says the source file was wrong; the other says the import process changed the value. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Trace a sample record from source through import, establish where the value changed, correct affected data, and agree a control at the actual failure point.$qb$, $qb$Tell the source team to resend the file.$qb$, $qb$Tell the importing team to rerun the process.$qb$, $qb$Ask both managers to agree who owns the error.$qb$, $qb$Correct the visible records manually and move on.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-142$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues are blaming each other by email after a mistake, and senior managers are being copied into increasingly critical messages. A correction still needs to be completed today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the issue to a short direct conversation, separate the urgent correction from the blame discussion, assign recovery actions, and schedule a factual cause review afterward.$qb$, $qb$Ask both colleagues to stop copying managers and focus only on the correction until the deadline is met.$qb$, $qb$Complete the correction yourself so their disagreement cannot delay the work.$qb$, $qb$Reply to the email chain with your view of who appears more responsible based on the evidence you have.$qb$, $qb$Forward the chain to both managers and ask them to resolve the disagreement before any further work continues.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1476$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about a late approval. One says the approver was not reminded; the other says reminders should not be necessary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the agreed approval process, timing and escalation triggers, resolve the current delay, and improve the mechanism rather than debating personal responsibility for remembering.$qb$, $qb$Support the person who sent the approval because approvers should remember.$qb$, $qb$Support the person who expected a reminder.$qb$, $qb$Ask the approver to decide who was at fault.$qb$, $qb$Add daily reminders for every approval.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1568$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree over whether to reopen a closed issue. One says the customer mentioned a similar symptom; the other says there is no proof it is the same cause. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the new symptom against the closure criteria and evidence, reopen or link the issue if warranted, and avoid assuming either sameness or independence without checking.$qb$, $qb$Reopen it because the symptom sounds similar.$qb$, $qb$Keep it closed because the original issue passed closure checks.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer whether they think it is the same issue.$qb$, $qb$Create a completely separate issue without comparing evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1614$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree about a customer escalation. One wants to apologize immediately; the other wants to investigate first because responsibility is unclear. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the customer's impact without claiming unverified fault, begin investigation, and give a clear time for the fuller response.$qb$, $qb$Apologize for causing the problem immediately.$qb$, $qb$Wait for full investigation before saying anything.$qb$, $qb$Let both teammates send separate messages.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer which approach they prefer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-188$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree over task ownership and have stopped sharing information. Their work must come together tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring them together, restate tomorrow's shared output, agree ownership of each remaining input, and set a short check-in so information starts flowing again.$qb$, $qb$Speak to each colleague separately and ask both to resume sharing what the other needs.$qb$, $qb$Reassign the disputed tasks yourself so the work can be combined on time.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to settle ownership before they continue.$qb$, $qb$Let each person complete their own part and combine whatever is available tomorrow.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-234$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two peers both believe the other changed an agreed requirement. Their discussion is becoming personal, but the team needs the correct requirement today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause the personal argument, review the recorded requirement and change history together, agree the current requirement, and schedule any responsibility discussion after the work is stabilized.$qb$, $qb$Ask each peer to send you their evidence separately and decide which version is correct.$qb$, $qb$Choose the version supported by the more senior peer so the team can proceed.$qb$, $qb$Tell both peers to stop arguing and continue with the requirement that was used most recently.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the disagreement to their managers before allowing the team to continue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-280$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues agree on the work that must be done but disagree strongly about the method, and the argument is delaying the start. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring the discussion back to the required outcome and constraints, compare the methods against those criteria, and agree a method or short test so work can begin.$qb$, $qb$Ask both colleagues to compromise by combining parts of their methods.$qb$, $qb$Choose the method proposed by the more experienced colleague.$qb$, $qb$Let each colleague use their own method on separate parts of the work.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the method disagreement to the manager before starting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2809$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teammates argue because one changed a shared file while the other was editing an older copy. Both have useful changes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause further edits, compare both versions, merge valid changes against the agreed source, and establish one version-control method before work resumes.$qb$, $qb$Keep the newer file and discard the older one.$qb$, $qb$Let each teammate merge their own changes into a third file.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide whose version should win.$qb$, $qb$Tell both teammates to start again from the last common version.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-326$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate criticizes another colleague's work in a group chat, and the colleague responds defensively. The discussion is quickly becoming personal. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the issue out of the public chat, restate the specific work concern, hear both perspectives, and agree the correction and future communication expectation privately.$qb$, $qb$Ask both colleagues in the chat to keep the discussion professional.$qb$, $qb$Delete or ignore the personal messages and focus only on correcting the work.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague who started the criticism to apologize publicly before continuing.$qb$, $qb$Forward the chat to the manager and let them handle the conflict.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-372$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teams both believe a shared deadline belongs to the other team, and the disagreement is becoming accusatory. The deadline is tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the agreed handoff and current work together, assign the remaining actions needed for tomorrow regardless of past ambiguity, and document future ownership after the immediate risk is controlled.$qb$, $qb$Decide which team appears more responsible and tell them to complete the work.$qb$, $qb$Split the remaining work equally between the teams.$qb$, $qb$Ask both team managers to settle responsibility before anyone proceeds.$qb$, $qb$Let the deadline slip so ownership can be clarified properly first.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-418$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague believes you changed a shared file without telling them. You believe they are looking at an older version. Their message is accusatory. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Respond calmly, compare the file history together, establish which version is current, and agree how future changes will be signaled before discussing responsibility.$qb$, $qb$Send a screenshot proving your version is newer and ask them to correct their accusation.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the tone and tell them which file to use.$qb$, $qb$Copy the file owner so they can determine who is right.$qb$, $qb$Reply that they should check version history before blaming colleagues.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-464$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A peer believes your team ignored their request; your team says the request was never received. Both sides are becoming defensive. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the actual request channel and records together, resolve the current need first, and agree a clearer receipt or acknowledgement method so future requests cannot disappear unnoticed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the peer to resend the request and move on.$qb$, $qb$Show that your team has no record and therefore cannot be responsible.$qb$, $qb$Tell both teams to copy managers on future requests.$qb$, $qb$Ask management to decide which team failed before doing the work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-510$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about who promised a customer an unrealistic date. The customer now needs a revised plan today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the immediate customer recovery from the responsibility dispute, agree a realistic revised plan first, then review the communication record afterward to learn how the promise occurred.$qb$, $qb$Review the messages first and identify who made the promise before contacting the customer.$qb$, $qb$Ask both colleagues to agree between themselves what new date to give the customer.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer an internal misunderstanding caused the problem and ask for more time.$qb$, $qb$Escalate both colleagues to their managers before proposing a revised date.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-556$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague believes you failed to include them in a decision; you believed another teammate was responsible for consulting them. They are angry and the decision is already made. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the missed involvement, hear what impact or information was lost, decide whether the decision needs revisiting, and clarify consultation ownership for future decisions.$qb$, $qb$Explain that another teammate was responsible for consulting them.$qb$, $qb$Apologize for the misunderstanding but keep the decision unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate who was responsible to resolve the complaint.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the decision cannot be reopened, so discussing consultation will not help.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-602$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teammates disagree over how a customer complaint was handled. One says the other ignored procedure; the other says the procedure did not fit the situation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the actual case and procedure together, separate whether the outcome was reasonable from whether an exception was properly handled, and agree any correction or process clarification.$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate who departed from procedure to follow it next time.$qb$, $qb$Support the person whose customer outcome was better.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide whether procedure or judgment should take priority.$qb$, $qb$End the disagreement because the customer issue is already closed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-740$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues accuse each other of failing to send a required file. One sent it to an old shared address; the other stopped monitoring that address months ago. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate file transfer, acknowledge the process gap on both sides, and agree one current channel with clear ownership so the same failure cannot recur.$qb$, $qb$Tell the sender they used the wrong address and therefore caused the problem.$qb$, $qb$Tell the receiver they should have kept monitoring the old address.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to decide who was responsible.$qb$, $qb$Ignore responsibility and simply resend the file each time this happens.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-786$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about whether a late change was properly approved. One has a chat message; the other has the formal tracker. Work is waiting. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the approval authority and both records, determine the valid current decision, restart work on that basis, and clarify where approvals must be recorded in future.$qb$, $qb$Use the formal tracker because formal records always override chat.$qb$, $qb$Use the chat because it is more recent.$qb$, $qb$Ask the two colleagues to agree which record they trust.$qb$, $qb$Pause all work until a manager investigates the approval history.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-832$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues disagree about whether a problem should be fixed now or documented for later. Their debate is becoming about who 'cares more about quality.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop the personal framing, agree the impact, urgency and cost criteria, assess the defect against them, and decide the timing based on those shared factors.$qb$, $qb$Support immediate fixing because quality problems should not be postponed.$qb$, $qb$Support later fixing because not every defect is urgent.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to choose between speed and quality.$qb$, $qb$Tell both colleagues to stop arguing and follow the existing backlog order.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-878$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues changed the same document section and each believes their version should remain. The deadline is near. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare both changes against the agreed objective and source evidence, combine compatible improvements, decide the true conflict quickly, and record the final rationale.$qb$, $qb$Keep the version edited most recently.$qb$, $qb$Use the version from the more experienced colleague.$qb$, $qb$Retain half of each edit as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Send both versions to the manager to choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-970$qb$, $qb$A3-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two teams changed the same shared process to solve different problems, and the changes now conflict. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring them together around the end-to-end outcome, identify what each change is solving, and agree one compatible design or explicit priority.$qb$, $qb$Keep the first change.$qb$, $qb$Roll back both.$qb$, $qb$Let each team use its own version.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior management to choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-005$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are coordinating a project task with a global team member whose primary language is different from yours. You notice slight misunderstandings regarding project deadlines in written chats. How do you adjust?$qb$, $qb$Transition key agreements to structured, plain-language written summaries with bulleted action items, clear dates, and explicitly invite clarification.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a quick video call to explain the project expectations verbally and answer any questions in real time.$qb$, $qb$Send follow-up reminder messages more frequently to make sure deadlines stay top of mind for the team member.$qb$, $qb$Reassign time-sensitive project tasks to a local team member while keeping non-urgent tasks with the global partner.$qb$, $qb$Continue using standard project chat channels, assuming team members will ask questions if they do not understand.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-097$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During an international call, a colleague says 'we should consider delaying' and your team interprets it as a formal recommendation to delay. Later you learn they meant only that delay was one possible option. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify decision language in the meeting, distinguish suggestions from recommendations or approvals, and record the agreed status in explicit terms.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to use stronger English next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team to be more careful with international accents.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the misunderstanding because the decision was corrected.$qb$, $qb$Use only written communication with that colleague in future.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1107$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project note says a decision is needed 'by end of day,' but contributors work in three time zones. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the phrase with an exact date, time and time zone, and use the same explicit convention for future cross-time-zone decisions.$qb$, $qb$Use the project manager's local end of day as the default.$qb$, $qb$Ask each contributor to respond by the end of their own day.$qb$, $qb$Keep the phrase because everyone knows the project manager's location.$qb$, $qb$Change it to 'as soon as possible.'$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1153$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate writes 'send this shortly' in a handoff to a colleague whose shift ends in 30 minutes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the exact required time and whether it must happen before the shift ends, then use explicit timing in the handoff.$qb$, $qb$Assume 'shortly' means within 30 minutes.$qb$, $qb$Tell the receiving colleague to use their judgment.$qb$, $qb$Change the word to 'soon.'$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the sender can clarify if asked.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1199$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells an overseas team that a task is due 'tomorrow morning,' but tomorrow is a public holiday in that team's location. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the actual required date and time with the local calendar in mind, then state the commitment explicitly rather than relying on relative wording.$qb$, $qb$Keep the deadline because the sender's calendar should apply.$qb$, $qb$Move it automatically to the day after the holiday.$qb$, $qb$Ask the overseas team to work on the holiday if urgent.$qb$, $qb$Leave it for the receiving team to interpret.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1245$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells a global team to finish a task 'before the weekend.' Some team members work Sunday to Thursday. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the phrase with an exact date and time, and use calendar-specific wording rather than assuming everyone shares the same weekend.$qb$, $qb$Clarify that weekend means Saturday and Sunday for this project.$qb$, $qb$Ask each team member to finish before their own weekend.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because everyone can see the sender's location.$qb$, $qb$Change the phrase to 'by Friday.'$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1291$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague writes 'please confirm at your earliest convenience' for a decision that must be made within four hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the polite but vague phrase with the exact decision deadline and explain any consequence of missing it.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording because it is professional and courteous.$qb$, $qb$Change it to 'urgent' without giving a time.$qb$, $qb$Send repeated reminders until the person responds.$qb$, $qb$Assume the recipient will understand the urgency from context.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1475$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague writes 'please action this' to an external partner who understands 'action' as a noun, not a request. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the phrase with the specific action required and the deadline, using direct plain language rather than local business shorthand.$qb$, $qb$Explain to the partner that 'action' is commonly used as a verb in business.$qb$, $qb$Keep the phrase and add 'urgent.'$qb$, $qb$Ask the partner to confirm what they think it means.$qb$, $qb$Use 'please do the needful' instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1521$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague writes 'please respond in the morning' to people in Asia, Europe and North America. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State the exact date, time and time zone for the response so everyone works to the same deadline.$qb$, $qb$Clarify that morning means the sender's morning.$qb$, $qb$Let each person respond during their own morning.$qb$, $qb$Change the wording to 'early tomorrow.'$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the recipients can see where the sender is based.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1567$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells an international team that a task is 'on the back burner.' Several people interpret it differently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the idiom with the actual priority, expected action and review date in plain language.$qb$, $qb$Explain that 'back burner' means low priority.$qb$, $qb$Change it to 'not urgent.'$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the team can ask questions.$qb$, $qb$Use another familiar idiom such as 'park it.'$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-233$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague uses a phrase in an email that some team members interpret as rude, but you know the colleague is communicating in a second language and may not intend that tone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Focus first on the work content, clarify the intended meaning privately if the tone is affecting collaboration, and avoid attributing motive based on wording alone.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the colleague probably did not mean to sound rude and move on.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to use softer language in future emails because others found the message offensive.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone from the colleague's cultural background whether the wording is normal there.$qb$, $qb$Forward the email to the manager because potentially disrespectful communication should be formally addressed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2349$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A global deadline is written as 10/11, and recipients interpret it as two different dates. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Rewrite it with the month in words plus exact time and time zone.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-279$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to repeat an instruction twice during a call because your speaking pace is difficult for them to follow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Slow your pace, use shorter sentences, summarize the action and date in writing, and invite them to confirm the next step.$qb$, $qb$Repeat the same instruction more loudly and slowly.$qb$, $qb$Send the written instruction after the call and continue speaking normally.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to explain the instruction after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Avoid giving the colleague time-sensitive work that requires verbal discussion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-325$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague uses unfamiliar technical abbreviations during a cross-functional meeting, and several non-specialists appear lost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for the key terms to be explained in plain language, summarize the decision in accessible wording, and encourage the group to avoid unexplained abbreviations in shared discussions.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to send a glossary after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Explain the abbreviations yourself whenever you know them.$qb$, $qb$Let the discussion continue because participants can ask if they do not understand.$qb$, $qb$Suggest that non-specialists attend a technical training course.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-371$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new colleague rarely asks questions in group meetings but sends several clarification messages afterward. You want to reduce avoidable rework without putting them on the spot. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Share key context before meetings, build pauses for questions, offer a written question channel, and check understanding of important actions without singling the colleague out.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague privately to raise questions during meetings so others can benefit too.$qb$, $qb$Continue answering their messages afterward because they may simply prefer written communication.$qb$, $qb$Call on the colleague directly at the end of every agenda item to ask whether they understand.$qb$, $qb$Tell their manager that the colleague needs to become more confident in meetings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-417$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague's written updates are technically correct but use idioms that several international teammates misunderstand. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Suggest using plain literal wording in shared updates, explain one or two examples of the confusion, and help establish clearer language for the whole team rather than criticizing the colleague's style.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to avoid all informal language in future messages.$qb$, $qb$Explain the idioms to teammates whenever confusion occurs.$qb$, $qb$Ask international teammates to tell the colleague directly when they do not understand.$qb$, $qb$Leave the writing unchanged because the updates are technically accurate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-463$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says 'yes' to a deadline in a group call but later privately explains that they thought saying no in front of senior people would be disrespectful. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify that realistic capacity concerns are welcome, revisit the deadline privately and with the relevant decision-makers, and use future planning language that explicitly invites constraints and alternatives.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague they should simply say no next time if a deadline is unrealistic.$qb$, $qb$Keep the deadline because they agreed to it in the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to confirm future deadlines on their behalf.$qb$, $qb$Avoid asking the colleague for commitments in group meetings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-509$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they understood your instructions, but their completed work suggests they interpreted one key term differently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the term using a concrete example, ask them to describe how they will apply it next time, and update shared wording if the term could confuse others too.$qb$, $qb$Correct their work and explain the definition they should have used.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to ask whenever they are unsure about terminology.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to review their future work for similar misunderstandings.$qb$, $qb$Use more technical detail next time so the meaning is precise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-555$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague often uses very direct wording that some teammates find uncomfortable, but the colleague appears unaware of the effect. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss a specific example privately, explain how the wording may be interpreted in the shared work context, and agree clearer alternatives without labeling the colleague's culture or personality.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague that teammates think their communication style is too blunt.$qb$, $qb$Ask teammates to be more tolerant because direct communication can be cultural.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the colleague's messages before they are sent.$qb$, $qb$Raise the issue with their manager rather than discussing it directly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-601$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague repeatedly uses the word 'urgent' for work that can wait several days. Other teammates have started treating all their messages as low priority. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the pattern privately, agree clearer urgency labels with concrete time expectations, and encourage the same shared convention across the team.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to stop using the word urgent unless something is due today.$qb$, $qb$Tell teammates which of the colleague's messages are genuinely urgent.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the wording and judge every request yourself.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to define which employees are allowed to mark work urgent.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-647$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a meeting, one colleague uses a local expression that another colleague interprets literally, leading to confusion about whether an action is optional. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the intended action in plain language, state whether it is required or optional, and use explicit wording in the written follow-up.$qb$, $qb$Explain the local expression to the colleague who misunderstood it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the speaker to avoid all local expressions in future meetings.$qb$, $qb$Let the colleagues resolve the misunderstanding between themselves.$qb$, $qb$Assume the written minutes will make the meaning clear later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-877$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A global team uses 'review' differently: some mean read and comment, others mean formal approval. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the required action explicitly—such as comment, recommend, or approve—and use those terms consistently.$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone review means read and comment unless approval is stated.$qb$, $qb$Ask each recipient what review means to them.$qb$, $qb$Let teams keep their own definitions.$qb$, $qb$Use 'review and approve' for every request.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-004$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A long-standing client asks for a 15% discount on their renewal contract. Granting this discount directly impacts your department's target margin. How do you negotiate?$qb$, $qb$Explore the client's primary goals, offer a phased service tier or modified deliverable schedule that maintains their budget while preserving target margins.$qb$, $qb$Offer a 7.5% compromise discount immediately to demonstrate goodwill and secure the renewal quickly.$qb$, $qb$Politely inform the client that contract prices are fixed according to standard company rate sheets, but offer extended payment terms.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the discount request directly to your department lead to decide if executive approval can be granted.$qb$, $qb$Agree to the 15% discount to prevent client churn, planning to absorb the margin loss through operational efficiencies.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder wants a report tomorrow with all requested analysis, but the available data can support either a quick directional view tomorrow or a validated full analysis two days later. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the decision they need to make, explain the reliability-time trade-off, and agree whether a directional view or later validated analysis better serves that decision.$qb$, $qb$Offer to send a short version tomorrow and the full version two days later.$qb$, $qb$Send the directional view tomorrow without discussing its limitations.$qb$, $qb$Refuse the request because a fully validated report cannot be ready tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Work late and try to complete the full analysis tomorrow even if validation is shortened.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-096$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks for three custom changes within the existing price. Your team can deliver one without harming the plan, but the other two require additional effort. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which changes create the most value, explain the effort and commercial impact, and agree a package that includes the highest-value change while pricing or deferring the others.$qb$, $qb$Agree to one change and reject the other two.$qb$, $qb$Accept all three changes to protect the customer relationship.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales to handle the request because it affects price.$qb$, $qb$Delay responding until the project is nearly finished.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1106$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a lower unit price if you accept a longer contract. Technology changes may reduce your need for the service before the contract ends. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the saving with exit risk and expected future need, then negotiate break clauses, volume flexibility, or a shorter commitment that preserves enough value.$qb$, $qb$Take the lower unit price because current demand is strong.$qb$, $qb$Reject any longer contract because future technology is uncertain.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the lower price with no commitment change.$qb$, $qb$Sign the long contract and plan to renegotiate if technology changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1152$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor offers free upgrades if you accept automatic renewal for two years. Your future usage may fall after a planned system replacement. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the upgrade value and future usage risk, then negotiate renewal notice, exit rights, or a shorter commitment before accepting the offer.$qb$, $qb$Accept because free upgrades reduce future cost.$qb$, $qb$Reject automatic renewal under all circumstances.$qb$, $qb$Ask for free upgrades without changing the contract.$qb$, $qb$Accept now and cancel later if usage falls.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1198$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a lower price if invoices are paid in 10 days instead of 30. Cash flow is tight during two months of the year. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Calculate the discount against financing and seasonal cash-flow impact, then negotiate seasonal terms or selective early payment where the net benefit is positive.$qb$, $qb$Accept because paying early always saves money.$qb$, $qb$Reject because the standard term is safer for cash flow.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the discount while keeping 30 days.$qb$, $qb$Accept and borrow money during the tight months if needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1290$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a lower fee if you waive the right to reduce service volume during the year. Your business is entering an uncertain market. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Value the discount against demand uncertainty, then negotiate a volume floor, step-down right, or other flexibility that limits unused commitment.$qb$, $qb$Accept because lower fixed fees improve the budget.$qb$, $qb$Reject the offer because future demand is uncertain.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the discount while retaining full flexibility.$qb$, $qb$Accept and plan to find extra uses for the service if demand falls.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-140$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor can meet your full delivery date only for a significant express fee. Half the order is critical this week; the rest can arrive next week without impact. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the urgent and non-urgent quantities, negotiate express delivery only for the critical portion, and keep standard delivery and pricing for the remainder.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for a reduced express fee on the full order and accept a reasonable compromise.$qb$, $qb$Pay the full express fee to protect the original date, then look for savings elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Insist on the original price and deadline because the order was placed on time.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the order and switch suppliers immediately without first confirming an alternative can meet quality and timing needs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1612$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a lower annual fee if you pay in one currency, but revenue for the service is earned mainly in another currency. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the fee saving with exchange-rate exposure and negotiate currency, adjustment, or payment terms based on net value.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lower fee because the saving is visible today.$qb$, $qb$Reject the offer because exchange rates can move.$qb$, $qb$Accept and convert currency only when invoices fall due.$qb$, $qb$Ignore currency exposure because it is a finance issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-186$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks for an additional deliverable at no extra cost. Your team can include it only by reducing another part of the agreed work or moving the date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which additional outcome matters most, show the effort and schedule impact, and offer choices to replace existing scope, move the date, or price the addition.$qb$, $qb$Offer the additional deliverable at a reduced charge as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Include it this time to protect the relationship and recover the effort elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Reject the request because the signed scope should not change.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide whether the customer is important enough for an exception.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2118$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a lower price if you pay 45 days earlier than normal terms. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the saving with cash-flow and financing impact, then negotiate the best net-value combination.$qb$, $qb$Agree to the counterparty's primary request in exchange for minor concessions on administrative terms.$qb$, $qb$Reject the counterparty's proposal outright without exploring mutually beneficial alternatives.$qb$, $qb$Accept unfavorable commercial terms immediately and plan to renegotiate during implementation.$qb$, $qb$Concede on major pricing and risk conditions to secure agreement quickly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2807$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a 6% discount if you accept quarterly deliveries instead of monthly deliveries. Your storage space is limited. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Calculate the discount against storage, handling and inventory risk, then negotiate delivery quantities or timing that preserve net value.$qb$, $qb$Take the discount because 6% is a clear saving.$qb$, $qb$Keep monthly delivery and reject the offer immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the discount with monthly delivery and make no other proposal.$qb$, $qb$Accept quarterly delivery and find extra storage later if needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-324$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor cannot reduce price further but offers either faster support response or a longer warranty at the same price. Both have value to your team. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which risk matters more over the contract period, quantify the likely value of each concession, and negotiate the option or combination that best protects that need.$qb$, $qb$Choose faster support because immediate service problems are easier to measure.$qb$, $qb$Choose the longer warranty because it provides value for more time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to decide which concession is easier for them to provide.$qb$, $qb$Reject both because neither reduces the purchase price.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-370$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier will improve delivery time if you accept less frequent status reporting. Your team values both speed and visibility. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify why reporting consumes supplier capacity, identify which updates are genuinely decision-critical, and negotiate a lighter reporting schedule that preserves essential visibility while enabling faster delivery.$qb$, $qb$Accept fewer reports because delivery speed directly affects the customer.$qb$, $qb$Keep current reporting and accept the slower delivery.$qb$, $qb$Ask for faster delivery while keeping all current reports because both are part of good service.$qb$, $qb$Let the supplier choose the reporting frequency if they guarantee the delivery date.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-416$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A contractor can start your work next week only if you accept fewer review checkpoints. Waiting for the normal checkpoints delays the start by three weeks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which checkpoints protect material risks, keep those, and negotiate lighter or combined reviews for lower-risk stages so the earlier start does not remove essential control.$qb$, $qb$Accept fewer checkpoints because the three-week delay is more damaging.$qb$, $qb$Keep every checkpoint and accept the later start.$qb$, $qb$Ask the contractor to start early while promising to complete missed reviews afterward.$qb$, $qb$Escalate to procurement leadership because the contractor should not link timing to review requirements.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-508$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers free installation if you accept delivery one week earlier than planned. Your site can receive the equipment early, but storage and security will cost money. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the installation saving with storage and security cost, explore whether delivery or installation can be separated, and negotiate the combination with the best total value.$qb$, $qb$Accept the early delivery because free installation is a visible saving.$qb$, $qb$Keep the planned delivery and pay for installation.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to provide free installation without changing the delivery date.$qb$, $qb$Reject the offer because changing delivery creates unnecessary complexity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-554$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor can hold your current price for another year only if you accept a minimum annual purchase. Your expected demand is close to, but not guaranteed to reach, that minimum. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Model realistic demand ranges and the cost of unused commitment, then negotiate a lower minimum, volume bands, or another mechanism that preserves price without excessive downside.$qb$, $qb$Accept the minimum because expected demand is close to it.$qb$, $qb$Reject the minimum and accept a higher price for full flexibility.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to keep the price without any volume commitment.$qb$, $qb$Let finance decide because the issue depends on future demand.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-600$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers priority delivery if you agree to consolidate all orders into one monthly shipment. Some of your demand is predictable, but urgent needs can arise mid-month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate predictable and urgent demand, negotiate consolidated shipping for the stable portion and an agreed exception route for genuine urgent needs.$qb$, $qb$Accept monthly consolidation because priority delivery improves reliability.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current ordering method because urgent needs make consolidation too risky.$qb$, $qb$Ask for priority delivery without changing your ordering pattern.$qb$, $qb$Agree to consolidation and use another supplier whenever an urgent need appears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-646$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier can reduce setup cost if your team accepts a fixed implementation date. Your internal readiness date may still move by a week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the saving against the cost of missing the fixed date, and negotiate a date window, limited rescheduling right, or staged setup that preserves value without creating disproportionate risk.$qb$, $qb$Accept the fixed date because the setup saving is guaranteed.$qb$, $qb$Reject the saving and keep full scheduling flexibility.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the discount while keeping unlimited date changes.$qb$, $qb$Wait until your internal date is certain before negotiating further.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-876$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A vendor offers free training only on fixed dates, while your teams need some scheduling flexibility. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Estimate the training value and scheduling risk, then negotiate limited rescheduling, mixed fixed and flexible sessions, or another arrangement preserving most of the benefit.$qb$, $qb$Accept the fixed dates because training is free.$qb$, $qb$Pay for training to choose every date.$qb$, $qb$Ask for free training with unlimited rescheduling.$qb$, $qb$Accept the dates and make teams rearrange work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-968$qb$, $qb$A3-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a lower rate if you accept a fixed monthly minimum, but your demand is seasonal. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Model seasonal demand and underuse cost, then negotiate seasonal bands or an annualized commitment that captures savings without excessive unused capacity.$qb$, $qb$Accept the minimum because annual demand is high.$qb$, $qb$Reject the offer because demand varies.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the rate without commitment.$qb$, $qb$Use average monthly demand as the minimum.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-003$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need to convince two peer team leads to adopt a standardized document-sharing format that requires 30 minutes of setup from their teams. They are reluctant due to current workloads. How do you influence them?$qb$, $qb$Highlight how the new format reduces their teams' weekly tracking effort by two hours, present a quick demo, and offer to assist with initial setup.$qb$, $qb$Present the operational compliance guidelines established by management that recommend adopting standardized file formats.$qb$, $qb$Offer to handle the manual document reformatting for their teams for the first month if they agree to adopt the format afterwards.$qb$, $qb$Ask your direct manager to mention the importance of standard formatting during the next joint leadership meeting.$qb$, $qb$Implement the standard format within your own team only and wait for peers to notice the efficiency gains independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A peer team resists using a shared checklist because they believe it adds unnecessary work. You need their cooperation for a joint deliverable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show specific rework caused by missed checks, ask about their concerns, and agree a short trial that measures whether the checklist reduces total effort.$qb$, $qb$Explain the checklist benefits and ask them to use it for the next deliverable.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the checklist is the best-practice approach and should be followed.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to require the other team to use the checklist.$qb$, $qb$Use the checklist only within your team and accept the handoff problems.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-095$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You believe a project team should spend one extra hour on a pre-launch review. Colleagues want to skip it because the schedule is tight. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use evidence from likely failure points, explain the cost of avoidable defects, and propose a focused review limited to the highest-risk areas.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the review is important and ask everyone to stay one hour longer.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project lead to make the review mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Skip the review but document that you disagreed.$qb$, $qb$Review the work alone after everyone leaves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1105$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to remove a data-validation step because only a small percentage of records fail it. Those failures can cause incorrect customer charges. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the failure frequency together with the financial and customer impact, then propose a targeted automated or risk-based validation rather than removing the protection entirely.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current validation because any billing error is unacceptable.$qb$, $qb$Remove it because the failure percentage is small.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client's finance leader to decide whether the risk is acceptable.$qb$, $qb$Keep validating only when staff think a record looks unusual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1151$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to cut testing from three days to one to protect a launch date. Most defects in earlier projects appeared on the second or third day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the defect history to show what risk the shorter test misses, then propose a focused test plan or launch adjustment that protects the highest-risk scenarios.$qb$, $qb$Refuse any reduction because three days is the standard.$qb$, $qb$Accept one day because the launch date is important.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to decide between quality and schedule.$qb$, $qb$Run one day of testing and fix later defects after launch.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1197$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to remove a reconciliation because it has found no differences for six months, but transaction volume will triple after launch. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain how the risk changes with higher volume, review whether the control can be automated or sampled, and recommend any reduction only after considering the new exposure.$qb$, $qb$Remove it because six months of clean results prove it is unnecessary.$qb$, $qb$Keep it unchanged forever because controls should not depend on volume.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client's auditor to decide.$qb$, $qb$Remove it for launch and restore it only if an error appears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1243$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer wants to skip a backup rehearsal because the primary service has never failed during their busiest season. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the business impact of a peak-period failure to explain why recovery readiness still matters, and propose a short rehearsal focused only on the critical recovery path.$qb$, $qb$Insist on the full standard rehearsal because backups must always be tested.$qb$, $qb$Agree to skip it because the service has a strong record.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer's executive sponsor to require the rehearsal.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the service could fail at any time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1289$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to remove a second-person check from high-value refunds because it slows response. Recent errors are rare but expensive. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show both error frequency and financial exposure, then propose faster routing or risk thresholds while retaining independent checking where the consequence justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Keep the second-person check for every refund because it prevents errors.$qb$, $qb$Remove it because errors are rare.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance leadership to make the decision.$qb$, $qb$Let experienced employees skip the check whenever they feel confident.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1381$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to reduce a pilot from 100 users to 10 to save time. The main risk is whether the process works across several different user groups. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that sample diversity matters more than raw size for this risk, and propose a smaller but representative pilot that includes the key user groups.$qb$, $qb$Accept 10 users chosen from the easiest group.$qb$, $qb$Insist on exactly 100 users because that was the original plan.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client to choose whichever users are available first.$qb$, $qb$Skip the pilot because a very small sample will not prove anything.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-139$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Three peers resist using a shared handover checklist because each already has a personal method. You believe the lack of a common checklist is causing repeated omissions between teams. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show two recent omissions linked to inconsistent handovers, demonstrate a short checklist that avoids duplicating their own notes, and propose a two-week trial with a review.$qb$, $qb$Explain that a common checklist will improve consistency and ask everyone to start using it from next week.$qb$, $qb$Offer to complete the shared checklist yourself after receiving each peer's handover.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to make the checklist mandatory because voluntary adoption is unlikely.$qb$, $qb$Use the checklist only for your own handovers and wait for others to copy the approach.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1427$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to remove contingency after early locations launched smoothly, but the remaining locations use older equipment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that the remaining sites have a different equipment risk and release contingency only as that specific risk is retired.$qb$, $qb$Remove contingency because earlier sites were smooth.$qb$, $qb$Keep all contingency until the entire rollout ends.$qb$, $qb$Move the contingency to another budget line.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to decide based only on current spend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1565$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to reduce password-reset verification because it adds time to calls. Fraud attempts are uncommon but can expose customer accounts. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the low frequency but high consequence of account takeover, then propose faster approved verification methods rather than weakening identity assurance.$qb$, $qb$Keep every current verification step because security should never change.$qb$, $qb$Remove a step because fraud attempts are uncommon.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client's security leader to decide.$qb$, $qb$Let experienced agents shorten verification when the caller sounds genuine.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-185$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your peers want to postpone a short pre-release check to save time. Recent releases show that this check catches issues that are expensive to fix later. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show recent examples of costly issues the check caught, compare its small time cost with later rework, and propose keeping a focused version for this release.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the check is good practice and ask the team to keep it.$qb$, $qb$Offer to perform the check yourself so everyone else can save time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the release manager to make the check compulsory.$qb$, $qb$Agree to skip it this time and revisit the decision only if problems occur.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-231$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client sponsor wants to launch a change immediately, while your usage data suggests a small pilot would expose adoption problems before a full rollout. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the adoption risks in the data, explain how a short pilot protects the sponsor's launch objective, and propose a tightly time-boxed pilot with clear success criteria.$qb$, $qb$Recommend a pilot and explain that gradual launches are generally safer.$qb$, $qb$Agree to the full launch but ask for additional support staff in case adoption problems appear.$qb$, $qb$Ask your senior leader to persuade the sponsor because the sponsor has already rejected your concern.$qb$, $qb$Stop challenging the full launch because the sponsor owns the business decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-369$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer is unwilling to change a reporting habit because the current method has never caused a serious problem. You know it creates a growing risk as volumes increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the customer's projected volume to show how the current risk scales, illustrate a realistic failure scenario, and propose a low-effort change before the risk becomes costly.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the current method is not best practice and recommend the new one.$qb$, $qb$Wait until a small problem occurs so you have concrete evidence to persuade them.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer's senior sponsor to mandate the change.$qb$, $qb$Accept the current method because the customer has not experienced harm yet.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-461$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer team resists documenting decisions because they believe meeting notes create unnecessary administration. Recent disagreements have come from people remembering decisions differently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show a recent disagreement caused by missing records, propose a very short decision log containing only decisions, owners and dates, and trial it for the next few meetings.$qb$, $qb$Explain that documenting decisions is good governance and ask the team to begin doing it.$qb$, $qb$Offer to write all meeting notes yourself so the customer team has no extra work.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer sponsor to require written minutes.$qb$, $qb$Accept verbal decisions because forcing documentation may damage the relationship.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-645$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to postpone cleaning old data before a system migration because the cleanup does not appear to affect launch. You know poor data will create problems after migration. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show examples of how the old data will appear or behave after migration, quantify the likely downstream rework, and propose a focused cleanup of the highest-impact records before launch.$qb$, $qb$Explain that data cleanup is standard good practice before migration.$qb$, $qb$Agree to migrate first and clean the data afterward.$qb$, $qb$Ask the client's sponsor to make cleanup mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to proceed with migration until every old record is cleaned.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-691$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer does not want to involve frontline users in a pilot because managers already understand the process. You believe user behavior is a major adoption risk. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain which assumptions can only be tested with actual users, show the cost of discovering adoption problems after launch, and propose a small representative user group with limited time commitment.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer that user involvement is a standard part of good pilots.$qb$, $qb$Proceed with managers only and add user testing after launch.$qb$, $qb$Ask the executive sponsor to require frontline participation.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to run the pilot unless frontline users participate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-737$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer wants to skip a short backup test because the primary system has been stable for months. A failure during their peak day would be costly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the peak-day impact to explain why backup readiness matters despite recent stability, and propose a brief test that verifies the critical recovery path with minimal disruption.$qb$, $qb$Explain that backup testing is standard risk management.$qb$, $qb$Agree to skip the test because recent stability lowers the risk.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer's senior sponsor to insist on the test.$qb$, $qb$Run the backup test without involving the customer team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-783$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to launch a promotion nationally after a successful test in one city. The city differs substantially from several other target markets. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show which conditions made the city test successful, identify where other markets differ, and propose a staged expansion that tests the most important differences before national rollout.$qb$, $qb$Recommend national launch because the first test was successful.$qb$, $qb$Ask for a second city test without explaining why it matters.$qb$, $qb$Tell the client that national rollout is too risky.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior leadership on both sides to decide the rollout scope.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-829$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to remove a contingency budget because the project has gone smoothly so far. The highest-risk phase has not started. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show which risks remain concentrated in the upcoming phase, explain what the contingency protects, and propose reducing or releasing it only as those risks retire.$qb$, $qb$Tell the client contingency should never be removed before project completion.$qb$, $qb$Agree to remove half as a compromise.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to protect the contingency budget.$qb$, $qb$Remove it because current performance shows the project is well controlled.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-921$qb$, $qb$A3-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client wants to skip preventive maintenance because equipment has been stable and downtime is inconvenient.$qb$, $qb$Compare planned downtime with failure impact using evidence and propose a low-disruption maintenance window.$qb$, $qb$Say maintenance is best practice.$qb$, $qb$Agree to postpone it.$qb$, $qb$Ask their sponsor to require it.$qb$, $qb$Warn that failure is likely soon.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-FIB-001$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Saying back what you understood in your own words lets the speaker correct any ______.$qb$, $qb$misunderstanding$qb$, $qb$figure$qb$, $qb$detail$qb$, $qb$opinion$qb$, $qb$delay$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-FIB-002$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Waiting a moment after someone stops speaking often lets them add the part they were ______.$qb$, $qb$holding$qb$, $qb$building$qb$, $qb$avoiding$qb$, $qb$repeating$qb$, $qb$answering$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
