  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-FIB-017$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Repeating a speaker's own words back to them shows you followed without adding your own ______.$qb$, $qb$opinion$qb$, $qb$summary$qb$, $qb$advice$qb$, $qb$concern$qb$, $qb$heading$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer at a phone shop is describing a fault. Halfway through, you already think you know what is wrong. What is the best thing to do?$qb$, $qb$Let them finish, since the detail they add last may change what you concluded$qb$, $qb$Let them finish and then check your conclusion by asking them one question$qb$, $qb$Let them finish out of courtesy, then explain the fault as you understand it$qb$, $qb$Tell them what you think the fault is, so their time is not wasted describing it$qb$, $qb$Ask a closed question to confirm your conclusion and move on to fixing it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team member says a task is 'nearly done' and then keeps talking about the difficulties they hit. What are they most likely telling you?$qb$, $qb$That the task is not as close to done as the first three words suggested$qb$, $qb$That they want the difficulties recognised as well as the progress made$qb$, $qb$That something in the task is still unresolved and they are working up to it$qb$, $qb$That they are explaining why it took longer than was originally expected$qb$, $qb$That the task is essentially finished and the detail is background information$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a ward handover, a nurse mentions a patient detail in passing and moves straight on. You are not sure it was important. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to go back to it, since a detail mentioned and dropped may still matter$qb$, $qb$Note it down and ask about it once the handover has finished completely$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the detail changes anything about the patient's care today$qb$, $qb$Assume it was minor, since an experienced nurse would have stopped for it$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether anyone else in the handover raises the same point$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague is explaining a problem and becomes upset partway through. What is the best thing to do?$qb$, $qb$Slow down and let them continue at their pace, without steering them back to facts$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge how they are feeling, then let them carry on when they are ready$qb$, $qb$Offer to continue the conversation at a later time when they feel calmer$qb$, $qb$Move the conversation back to the facts, since that is what you can help with$qb$, $qb$Reassure them that the problem is smaller than it feels and continue as before$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are taking an order over the phone in a busy kitchen. The customer keeps changing their mind. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Read the whole order back at the end, so both of you are working from the same list$qb$, $qb$Confirm each change as it comes, so nothing is carried forward incorrectly$qb$, $qb$Write down every version, then ask which one they want to go with$qb$, $qb$Ask them to decide first and call back when the order is settled$qb$, $qb$Take the last version they said, since that is what they most recently wanted$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A trainee asks a question you have already answered twice this week. What is the most useful response?$qb$, $qb$Answer it differently this time, since the first two explanations clearly did not land$qb$, $qb$Ask what part is still unclear, so the gap can be found rather than guessed at$qb$, $qb$Answer it again patiently, since repetition is a normal part of learning a job$qb$, $qb$Point them to where the answer is written down so they can look it up$qb$, $qb$Remind them that this has been covered and ask them to make notes next time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A supplier on a call says 'that should be fine' when asked about a delivery date. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what would have to happen for the date to slip, and who would tell you$qb$, $qb$Ask them to confirm the date directly, so the answer is a yes or a no$qb$, $qb$Ask what their confidence is based on and whether anything could change it$qb$, $qb$Take it as agreement, and follow up in writing to confirm the date$qb$, $qb$Take it as a yes, since a supplier would say so if there were a problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a team meeting, a quieter colleague starts to speak and is talked over. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Bring the conversation back to them and ask them to continue their point$qb$, $qb$Note what they were saying and return to it before the topic moves on$qb$, $qb$Speak to them after the meeting and ask what they were going to say$qb$, $qb$Let the discussion continue, since they can speak again if it matters to them$qb$, $qb$Summarise what you think they meant so the group has heard the point$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer says 'it's fine, don't worry about it' but their tone suggests otherwise. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Check gently, since the words and the tone are not saying the same thing$qb$, $qb$Ask whether there is anything else about it they would like to raise$qb$, $qb$Say that you would rather sort it properly, and invite them to explain$qb$, $qb$Accept what they said, since pressing further might make them uncomfortable$qb$, $qb$Accept it and note the interaction in case they raise it again later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are interviewing a job candidate who gives a vague answer to a direct question. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for a specific example, so the answer becomes something you can actually assess$qb$, $qb$Ask the question again in a different way, in case the wording was unclear$qb$, $qb$Ask a follow-up about the part of the answer that was least clear to you$qb$, $qb$Move on and judge the vagueness as part of your view of the candidate$qb$, $qb$Accept the answer, since some candidates are nervous and answer generally$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A resident at a care home tells you the same story for the third time today. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Listen as though it is new, since the telling may matter more than the story$qb$, $qb$Listen and gently ask about a detail, to see whether something has changed$qb$, $qb$Listen briefly and then move the conversation to something else you can help with$qb$, $qb$Remind them kindly that they have already told you this earlier today$qb$, $qb$Let them continue while getting on with your other tasks in the room$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager gives you instructions quickly and asks if you understand. You are unsure about one step. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Say back the steps as you understood them, so the unclear one comes out naturally$qb$, $qb$Ask directly about the step you are unsure of before the conversation ends$qb$, $qb$Say you will check the unclear step with a colleague after the meeting$qb$, $qb$Say you understand and work the step out yourself once you begin the task$qb$, $qb$Say you understand, since asking now would take up more of their time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two colleagues are arguing and each keeps repeating their own point. What does the repetition suggest?$qb$, $qb$Neither feels the other has actually taken in what they are saying$qb$, $qb$Both are more focused on being understood than on understanding$qb$, $qb$The argument is stuck because no new information is being added$qb$, $qb$Both are convinced they are right and neither will change position$qb$, $qb$The argument needs someone else to decide which of them is correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer explains a complicated complaint. You need to check you have understood before responding. What is the best way?$qb$, $qb$Summarise it back and ask whether you have missed or changed anything$qb$, $qb$Repeat the main points and ask them to confirm each one in turn$qb$, $qb$Ask a few questions about the parts you are least sure you followed$qb$, $qb$Explain your understanding and then move on to what you can do about it$qb$, $qb$Ask them to put the complaint in writing so nothing is misunderstood$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During a site briefing, a worker says the new method 'might be tricky in the rain'. Nobody responds. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to say more, since a passing comment may be a real safety concern$qb$, $qb$Raise it directly with the group, since weather affects everyone on the site$qb$, $qb$Ask them after the briefing what they meant and whether it needs raising$qb$, $qb$Note it and see whether it becomes a problem once the work is underway$qb$, $qb$Let it pass, since they would have pressed the point if it were serious$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A parent at a school reception is describing a concern and keeps going back to the beginning. What is happening?$qb$, $qb$They are likely still working out what the main concern actually is$qb$, $qb$They may not feel that the concern has been understood or taken on yet$qb$, $qb$They may have several linked concerns that have not been separated out$qb$, $qb$They are probably repeating themselves out of frustration with the situation$qb$, $qb$They are giving background so the concern makes sense to the listener$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are on a video call and a colleague's point is cut off by a connection problem. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to start that point again from the beginning once they reconnect$qb$, $qb$Tell them where you lost them and ask them to pick up from that point$qb$, $qb$Ask whether they finished the point or whether there was more to come$qb$, $qb$Summarise what you heard and ask whether that was the whole point$qb$, $qb$Carry on, since they can raise it again if the point still matters$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work on a supermarket checkout. An elderly customer is trying to explain a problem with a discount, but keeps losing track and starting again. There is a queue building behind her. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Give her your attention and ask one question at a time, so the problem can be found$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to open another till, then give her the time she needs to explain$qb$, $qb$Ask her the two or three questions most likely to get to the discount problem$qb$, $qb$Refund the discount to move things along, and suggest she checks it at home$qb$, $qb$Explain that there is a queue and ask her to speak to customer service instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member has asked to speak with you about a problem. Ten minutes in, you realise they have not yet reached the point, and you have another meeting shortly. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Tell them the time you have and ask what the main thing is, so it gets covered$qb$, $qb$Let them continue and arrange more time, since cutting in may lose the real point$qb$, $qb$Ask a question that helps them get to the point without making them feel rushed$qb$, $qb$Summarise what you think the problem is and check whether you have it right$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send it in writing so you can read it properly before you meet$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are training a new colleague at a building materials shop. They nod at everything you say but then make the same mistake twice. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the step back to you, so you can hear where it breaks down$qb$, $qb$Ask what part they find hardest, since nodding has not shown you what is unclear$qb$, $qb$Show them the step again and watch them do it before leaving them to it$qb$, $qb$Explain the step more slowly, since they may not have taken it in the first time$qb$, $qb$Point out that they said they understood, and ask them to speak up next time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer has phoned to complain. They spend several minutes on background before reaching the issue. You already know from the account what the problem is. How do you handle the call?$qb$, $qb$Let them finish, since being heard is part of what they phoned about$qb$, $qb$Listen fully and then confirm the problem, so they know you followed it$qb$, $qb$Listen and ask one question that moves them toward the issue politely$qb$, $qb$Tell them you can see the problem on the account and offer the fix now$qb$, $qb$Explain that you already know the issue, so their time is not spent on background$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In a handover at a care home, a colleague mentions that a resident 'has been a bit different today' and then moves on to the next resident. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask what they noticed, then make sure it reaches the record before they leave$qb$, $qb$Ask them to describe what was different, so you know what to watch for$qb$, $qb$Ask whether anything has changed in the resident's medication or routine$qb$, $qb$Note it in the record and watch the resident yourself during your shift$qb$, $qb$Let it pass, since your colleague would have said more if it concerned them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are leading a team meeting. One person is dominating and two others have not spoken. The topic affects everyone equally. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Thank the speaker and ask the others directly what their view is$qb$, $qb$Ask each person in turn for a brief view, so everyone is heard once$qb$, $qb$Ask the quieter people a specific question they are well placed to answer$qb$, $qb$Ask the dominant speaker to allow others in, so the discussion opens up$qb$, $qb$Let it run, since anyone who wants to speak in the meeting is free to do so$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague comes to you upset about a decision. You believe the decision was correct. How do you handle the conversation?$qb$, $qb$Hear the whole objection first, since they may know something you do not$qb$, $qb$Listen fully and ask what specifically about the decision troubles them most$qb$, $qb$Listen, then explain the reasoning behind the decision as you understand it$qb$, $qb$Explain the reasoning early, so they can see the decision was not unfair$qb$, $qb$Tell them the decision is made and suggest they raise it with the manager$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a receptionist at a garage. A customer describes a noise their car is making, using words that do not match any technical term. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Ask when the noise happens and what it sounds like, in their own words$qb$, $qb$Write down exactly how they described it, so the mechanic gets their words$qb$, $qb$Ask them to make the sound or point to where in the car it comes from$qb$, $qb$Translate it into the closest technical term so the mechanic understands it$qb$, $qb$Book the car in and let the mechanic work out what the noise actually is$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier is explaining why an order is late. You notice they keep saying 'we were told' rather than naming who told them. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask who told them and when, since the wording suggests the account is incomplete$qb$, $qb$Ask them to take you through the sequence of events step by step$qb$, $qb$Ask what they will do differently, since the explanation is not becoming clearer$qb$, $qb$Accept the explanation and focus the conversation on the new delivery date$qb$, $qb$Accept it, since pressing a supplier on detail may damage the relationship$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague raises an idea in a meeting. It is not well explained and the group moves on quickly. You think there may be something in it. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Bring it back and ask them to explain the problem the idea was solving$qb$, $qb$Ask them to say more, so the group hears the idea properly before deciding$qb$, $qb$Speak to them afterwards and ask them to explain it to you in full$qb$, $qb$Explain the idea to the group yourself, in clearer terms than they used$qb$, $qb$Let it go, since a good idea will be raised again if it really matters$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are dealing with a customer whose English is limited. They are becoming frustrated because you have asked them to repeat themselves twice. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Slow your own speech and check understanding in small steps rather than asking again$qb$, $qb$Say back what you have understood so far, so they only fill in the gap$qb$, $qb$Ask shorter and simpler questions, so each answer is easier for them to give$qb$, $qb$Find a colleague who speaks their language and hand the conversation over$qb$, $qb$Ask them to write it down, since reading may be easier than understanding speech$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-APL-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A3-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In a shift briefing, a worker raises a concern and the supervisor answers a slightly different question. The worker does not press it. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Point out that the original question has not been answered, and restate it$qb$, $qb$Ask the worker whether their concern has been covered by that answer$qb$, $qb$Raise the same concern yourself, so it gets addressed in the briefing$qb$, $qb$Speak to the supervisor afterwards about the point that was not answered$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the worker chose not to press it and may be satisfied$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
