  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$Asking how something is normally done here is better than assuming it is done the same ______.$qb$, $qb$way$qb$, $qb$day$qb$, $qb$speed$qb$, $qb$order$qb$, $qb$reason$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-017$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new colleague's name is unfamiliar to you and you are unsure how to say it. What is best?$qb$, $qb$Ask her how she says it$qb$, $qb$Say it as best you can and let her correct you$qb$, $qb$Use a shortened version until you learn it$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague who has met her before$qb$, $qb$Avoid using her name until you are sure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a meeting, a colleague whose first language is not English pauses often mid-sentence. What is the most useful response?$qb$, $qb$Wait for him to finish his own sentences$qb$, $qb$Offer the word when he seems to be searching$qb$, $qb$Summarise what you think he means$qb$, $qb$Move on and speak to him about it afterwards$qb$, $qb$Ask him to send his points in writing instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are writing instructions that will be read by people in several countries. What helps most?$qb$, $qb$Avoiding phrases that only make sense locally$qb$, $qb$Keeping the sentences short$qb$, $qb$Adding a glossary of difficult terms$qb$, $qb$Offering to explain anything unclear$qb$, $qb$Having the instructions translated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague from another office keeps replying to your emails much more formally than you write. What does this most likely mean?$qb$, $qb$Formality means something different where he works$qb$, $qb$He does not know you well enough yet$qb$, $qb$He is being careful because it is in writing$qb$, $qb$He finds your emails too casual$qb$, $qb$He prefers written communication to be formal$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are booking a team lunch. What is the best approach to what is ordered?$qb$, $qb$Ask the team what they need before choosing the place$qb$, $qb$Choose a place with a wide range of dishes$qb$, $qb$Ask if anyone has dietary requirements$qb$, $qb$Choose somewhere and let people order for themselves$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to vote on where to go$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team member has asked for a day off for a religious festival you have not heard of. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Treat it like any other leave request$qb$, $qb$Ask him to explain what the festival is$qb$, $qb$Approve it and read about the festival afterwards$qb$, $qb$Check whether other staff have asked for the same day$qb$, $qb$Ask whether he could take a different day instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are leading a video meeting with people in three time zones. What matters most?$qb$, $qb$That the time chosen is not unreasonable for anyone$qb$, $qb$That the meeting is recorded for those who miss it$qb$, $qb$That everyone confirms they can make the time$qb$, $qb$That the meeting is kept as short as possible$qb$, $qb$That the time rotates between meetings$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague apologises far more than seems necessary to you. What is the most useful reading?$qb$, $qb$Apologising may carry a different weight for him$qb$, $qb$He may be less confident than the rest of the team$qb$, $qb$He may be worried about how his work is seen$qb$, $qb$It is a personal habit rather than anything more$qb$, $qb$He may be trying to avoid being blamed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have given feedback to a colleague and he agreed with everything you said. What is worth checking?$qb$, $qb$Whether agreeing was easier than disagreeing$qb$, $qb$Whether he understood all the points you made$qb$, $qb$Whether he intends to act on the feedback$qb$, $qb$Whether he had any feedback for you in return$qb$, $qb$Nothing, since he agreed with the feedback$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are explaining a task to someone whose English is limited. What is most likely to work?$qb$, $qb$Short sentences with one instruction in each$qb$, $qb$Speaking more slowly than you normally would$qb$, $qb$Writing the instructions down as well$qb$, $qb$Asking whether he has any questions$qb$, $qb$Demonstrating the task rather than explaining it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two team members from the same background always speak their own language together at work. Others have mentioned feeling excluded. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to agree when a shared language is needed$qb$, $qb$Ask the two to speak English when others are present$qb$, $qb$Explain to the others that private conversations are normal$qb$, $qb$Raise it with the two of them privately$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since people may speak as they choose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are working with a supplier abroad and the emails have become tense. Neither side seems to have done anything wrong. What is the most useful step?$qb$, $qb$Arrange a call so tone stops being guessed at$qb$, $qb$Write more carefully to avoid causing offence$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier whether something has gone wrong$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to read the emails for tone$qb$, $qb$Keep the emails short and factual from now on$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A candidate at interview does not make eye contact with you. What should you conclude?$qb$, $qb$Nothing about the candidate from that alone$qb$, $qb$That the candidate may be nervous$qb$, $qb$That eye contact norms vary between people$qb$, $qb$That the candidate may lack confidence$qb$, $qb$That the candidate is not engaged with the interview$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are running a training session for a group with a wide range of English levels. What is the most useful thing to do?$qb$, $qb$Check understanding at points during the session$qb$, $qb$Speak clearly and avoid unnecessary jargon$qb$, $qb$Provide written material alongside the session$qb$, $qb$Ask people to stop you if anything is unclear$qb$, $qb$Repeat each main point more than once$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says he will 'try' to have something ready by Friday. What is the most useful response?$qb$, $qb$Ask him what would stop it being ready$qb$, $qb$Ask him to confirm whether Friday is realistic$qb$, $qb$Take it as a yes and follow up on Thursday$qb$, $qb$Ask when he could be certain of finishing it$qb$, $qb$Accept it, since he has said he will try$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team has members who observe different rest days. How should recurring meetings be set?$qb$, $qb$Find the days that work for everyone before fixing them$qb$, $qb$Set them on the most common working days$qb$, $qb$Rotate the day so nobody always misses out$qb$, $qb$Set them and record the meeting for anyone absent$qb$, $qb$Ask anyone affected to raise it if there is a problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have misunderstood something a colleague from another country told you, and acted on it. What is the most useful step?$qb$, $qb$Check with him what he actually meant$qb$, $qb$Explain what you understood and where it went wrong$qb$, $qb$Ask him to put things in writing in future$qb$, $qb$Apologise for the misunderstanding and move on$qb$, $qb$Note it and be more careful next time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are chairing a meeting where some people speak far more than others. What is the most useful thing to consider?$qb$, $qb$Whether the format suits everyone equally$qb$, $qb$Whether the quieter people have been invited to speak$qb$, $qb$Whether the topic matters more to some than others$qb$, $qb$Whether the quieter people have less to contribute$qb$, $qb$Whether the meeting is running to time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-035$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A written message you sent has been read as blunt by the recipient. You did not intend that. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Say plainly what you meant and check it is settled$qb$, $qb$Apologise for how the message came across$qb$, $qb$Explain that you write briefly to everyone$qb$, $qb$Speak to him in person about something else first$qb$, $qb$Let it pass, since no offence was intended$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-MCQ-036$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most reliable way to find out how someone prefers to work with you?$qb$, $qb$Ask them$qb$, $qb$Watch how they work with others$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague who knows them$qb$, $qb$Try an approach and adjust it$qb$, $qb$Follow the usual practice in your team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a small warehouse team. A new member speaks limited English. On his second day he nodded when you explained a safety step, but you have just seen him do it wrongly. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Show him the step and ask him to do it back to you while you watch$qb$, $qb$Explain the step again, more slowly and in simpler words$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague who shares his language to explain it to him$qb$, $qb$Give him the written safety instructions to read$qb$, $qb$Tell him again and remind him to say if he does not understand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a hotel. A guest has complained about a member of your staff being rude. From what the guest describes, the exchange sounds like a difference in what counts as polite, not rudeness. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Take the complaint seriously with the guest, then talk it through with the staff member$qb$, $qb$Apologise to the guest and speak to the staff member about it$qb$, $qb$Explain to the guest that no rudeness was intended$qb$, $qb$Tell the staff member to be more careful with guests$qb$, $qb$Note the complaint but take no action, since nothing wrong was said$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You lead a project with a team in another country. Your counterpart there always agrees to deadlines in meetings, but the work often arrives late. How do you address this?$qb$, $qb$Ask what would make each deadline achievable before agreeing it$qb$, $qb$Ask him privately why the deadlines are being missed$qb$, $qb$Set earlier deadlines to allow for the slippage$qb$, $qb$Ask him to say clearly in meetings if a date is difficult$qb$, $qb$Escalate the pattern to his manager$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a teacher. A parent has come in and is speaking to you through her teenage son, who is translating. You need to discuss something about the son's own progress. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Arrange another meeting with an independent interpreter$qb$, $qb$Continue, but check with the parent that she has understood$qb$, $qb$Continue with the son translating, since he is willing$qb$, $qb$Write the points down for the parent to take away$qb$, $qb$Ask the parent to bring another adult next time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You supervise a mixed team. One member has told you that a running joke among the others makes him uncomfortable, but asked you not to say it came from him. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Raise the joke as something you noticed yourself and stop it$qb$, $qb$Tell the team that jokes of that kind are not acceptable here$qb$, $qb$Speak to the person who started the joke privately$qb$, $qb$Ask the team whether anyone finds the joke uncomfortable$qb$, $qb$Tell him to raise it himself if it bothers him$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work on a helpdesk. A caller is difficult to understand because of a poor line and an unfamiliar accent. You have asked her to repeat twice and she is becoming frustrated. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Say back what you have understood so far and ask her to fill the gap$qb$, $qb$Apologise for the line and ask her to repeat once more$qb$, $qb$Ask her to go through it again slowly, one point at a time$qb$, $qb$Ask whether she could email the details instead$qb$, $qb$Pass her to a colleague who may hear her better$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a shop. Two customers are speaking to each other in a language you do not understand while looking at an expensive item. A colleague suggests watching them closely. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Tell him plainly that the language they speak is not a reason to watch them$qb$, $qb$Say you will approach them and offer help as normal$qb$, $qb$Tell him the shop watches all customers the same way$qb$, $qb$Ask him what else made him suspicious about them$qb$, $qb$Agree to keep an eye on them since the item is expensive$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are recruiting. A strong candidate on paper interviewed poorly, and you think her spoken English may have been the barrier rather than her ability. The role involves little speaking. How do you proceed?$qb$, $qb$Assess her against what the job actually needs$qb$, $qb$Give her a second interview with more time$qb$, $qb$Ask her to complete a practical task instead$qb$, $qb$Discuss it with the other interviewer before deciding$qb$, $qb$Reject her, since the interview is the same test for everyone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a nurse. A patient's family have brought food from home and want to give it to him. Ward rules do not allow outside food. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Explain the rule and find out with them what can be allowed$qb$, $qb$Explain the ward rule and ask them to take the food home$qb$, $qb$Ask the ward manager whether an exception can be made$qb$, $qb$Allow it this once and mention the rule for next time$qb$, $qb$Tell them outside food is not permitted on the ward$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You lead a team spread across three countries. Your usual weekly meeting time falls during a national holiday in one country next week. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Move the meeting rather than have that country miss it$qb$, $qb$Hold it as normal and share the notes with them$qb$, $qb$Ask them whether they would prefer to move it$qb$, $qb$Hold it and arrange a separate catch-up with them$qb$, $qb$Hold it as normal, since one country cannot set the schedule$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in an office. A colleague has started fasting and is visibly tired in the afternoons. His work is unaffected. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Ask him whether anything about his hours would help$qb$, $qb$Offer to move your joint meetings to the mornings$qb$, $qb$Ask him whether he is managing all right$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since his work is not suffering$qb$, $qb$Suggest he takes leave if he is finding it hard$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are training a group where two members clearly know the subject already but have said nothing all morning. Others are asking questions freely. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Ask them a direct question they are well placed to answer$qb$, $qb$Invite anyone with experience of this to share it$qb$, $qb$Speak to them at the break about contributing$qb$, $qb$Continue, since they can speak if they want to$qb$, $qb$Ask them why they have not been participating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a site supervisor. Two workers who share a first language have been given a safety briefing in English. One of them has just explained something to the other in their own language and both look unsure. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Ask what part is unclear and go through it again with both$qb$, $qb$Ask the one who was explaining what he told the other$qb$, $qb$Repeat the whole briefing more slowly for the two of them$qb$, $qb$Ask them to confirm that they have understood the briefing$qb$, $qb$Carry on, since the briefing has been delivered to everyone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-LCS-SJT-050$qb$, $qb$A3-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You manage a customer service team. A customer has asked to be dealt with by someone other than the assistant currently helping him, and it is clear the reason is the assistant's accent. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the assistant will continue to deal with him$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer what the difficulty is with the current assistant$qb$, $qb$Deal with the customer yourself and support the assistant afterwards$qb$, $qb$Move the customer to someone else to settle the situation$qb$, $qb$Ask the assistant whether she minds handing the customer over$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true)
